OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026", "title": "Minimal models and Mori fibre spaces for generalized log canonical Q-pairs", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "036", "family_title": "Numerical semiampleness and generalized minimal models", "abstract_html": "We resolve the existence form of the minimal-model conjecture for projective generalized log canonical ℚ-pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero. Such a pair admits a minimal model when its adjoint divisor is pseudo-effective, and a Mori fibre space otherwise, while keeping the nef ℚ-Cartier b-divisor fixed. Taking the nef part to be zero gives the corresponding result for ordinary log canonical ℚ-pairs.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a normal integral projective variety \\(Y\\) and a birational map \\( \\phi:X\\dashrightarrow Y \\) whose inverse contracts no divisors such that, with \\(B_Y=\\phi_*B\\) and \\(M_Y\\) the trace of the same nef …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((X,B+M)\\) be a projective generalized log canonical \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-pair. Thus \\(X\\) is normal and integral, \\(B\\) is an effective rational boundary, and \\(M\\) is the trace of a nef rational Cartier divisor on a higher projective birational model, with \\(D=K_X+B+M\\) \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier.</p><p class=\"li\">The nef \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier b-divisor is fixed throughout.</p><p class=\"li\">On a smooth model \\(p:W\\to X\\) carrying the nef data, write\n  \\[\n  K_W+B_W+M_W=p^*(K_X+B+M),\n  \\qquad\n  A_{X,B+M}(E)=1-\\operatorname{coeff}_E(B_W).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a normal integral projective variety \\(Y\\) and a birational map\n\\[\n\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow Y\n\\]\nwhose inverse contracts no divisors such that, with \\(B_Y=\\phi_*B\\) and \\(M_Y\\) the trace of the same nef b-divisor, \\((Y,B_Y+M_Y)\\) is generalized log canonical and\n\\[\nA_{X,B+M}(E)\\le A_{Y,B_Y+M_Y}(E)\n\\]\nfor every prime divisor over the common function field. The inequality is strict for every divisor on \\(X\\) contracted by \\(\\phi\\).</p><p>More precisely:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(D\\) is pseudo-effective, then \\(K_Y+B_Y+M_Y\\) is nef.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(D\\) is not pseudo-effective, there is a projective contraction \\(h:Y\\to T\\) to a normal projective variety, with connected fibres, such that\n   \\[\n   \\dim T&lt;\\dim Y,\\qquad\n   \\rho(Y/T)=1,\\qquad\n   -(K_Y+B_Y+M_Y)\\ \\text{is ample over }T.\n   \\]</p><p>No \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factoriality is imposed on \\(X\\) or \\(Y\\). The assertion gives a terminating choice of birational program, not termination of every arbitrary MMP. The nef conclusion does not itself assert semiampleness or a nonzero pluricanonical section.</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 2–5 prove the bounded-fibre estimates. Propositions 6–7 give the tower reduction, Lifting, and Mixing. Proposition 8 proves the integral jump estimate. Propositions 9–10 yield the equivariant cone estimates and degree-product bound; Propositions 11–13 then prove the local phase assertion.</p><p>Applying that local assertion, Proposition 14 proves smooth absolute canonical termination. Proposition 15 supplies relative smooth NQC weak Zariski decompositions. Proposition 16 transfers these inputs to a finite generalized MMP over \\(\\mathbb C\\), checking the exact discrepancies, non-extraction, and Mori fibre rank. Proposition 17 gives the same conclusion over the stated algebraically closed field \\(k\\). These are precisely the assertions of Theorem 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2, Ordinary log canonical pairs", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a birational map \\(\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow Y\\) extracting no divisors, with \\(B_Y=\\phi_*B\\), such that \\((Y,B_Y)\\) is log canonical and \\( A_{X,B}(E)\\le A_{Y,B_Y}(E) \\) for every prime divisor over the common …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((X,B)\\) be a projective log canonical \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-pair over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Log discrepancies use the convention \\(A(E)=1-\\operatorname{coeff}_E(B_W)\\) on a crepant model.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a birational map \\(\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow Y\\) extracting no divisors, with \\(B_Y=\\phi_*B\\), such that \\((Y,B_Y)\\) is log canonical and\n\\[\nA_{X,B}(E)\\le A_{Y,B_Y}(E)\n\\]\nfor every prime divisor over the common function field, strictly for prime divisors on \\(X\\) contracted by \\(\\phi\\).</p><p>If \\(K_X+B\\) is pseudo-effective, \\(K_Y+B_Y\\) is nef. Otherwise \\(Y\\) admits a Mori fibre contraction with relative Picard number one and relatively ample \\(-(K_Y+B_Y)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Theorem 1 with the fixed zero nef b-divisor. The generalized crepant discrepancy equation becomes the ordinary one, and all output, strictness, nef, and Mori fibre assertions specialize to those stated.</p><p>As separately noted in the paper, the ordinary pseudo-effective endpoint also satisfies the hypotheses of the cited companion log abundance theorem, which makes \\(K_Y+B_Y\\) semiample there. This additional cited consequence makes no semiampleness assertion for nonzero generalized nef data.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 10.2, Ordinary base substitution, including the consequences recorded in equations (10.7)–(10.8)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The threshold canonical bundle formula gives a discriminant b-divisor and a b-Cartier b-semiample moduli b-divisor whose generalized discrepancy is \\(\\bar A_\\omega\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L/K\\) be a regular extension of finitely generated fields over \\(\\mathbb C\\), with a normal geometrically integral projective generic model \\(G/K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A form of index \\(q\\) is a nonzero rational section of the \\(q\\)-th tensor power of the absolute top differential line. Its boundary is minus its divisor divided by \\(q\\); on \\(G\\), divide out a base volume form.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\omega\\) have effective sub-lc boundary on \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonzero divisorial valuation \\(u\\) of \\(K\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\bar A_\\omega(u)=\\inf_{w|_K=u}A_\\omega(w),\n  \\]\n  allowing positive rational scalings of prime divisorial valuations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The threshold canonical bundle formula gives a discriminant b-divisor and a b-Cartier b-semiample moduli b-divisor whose generalized discrepancy is \\(\\bar A_\\omega\\). Every defining infimum is finite and attained.</p><p>If another such form \\(\\psi\\) satisfies \\(\\bar A_\\psi(u)&gt;0\\) for every nonzero divisorial \\(u\\), then for every rational \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) there is a form \\(\\omega_K\\) on \\(K\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\omega(u)-\\gamma\\bar A_\\psi(u)\n\\le A_{\\omega_K}(u)\\le\\bar A_\\omega(u).\n\\]\nNo index bound is asserted, and \\(\\omega\\) need not be sub-lc at vertical valuations.</p><p>If the carried boundary is effective on a specified source model over a normal base \\(U\\), every substitution satisfying \\(A_{\\omega_K}\\le\\bar A_\\omega\\) has effective trace on \\(U\\). Ordinary adjoint replacement is an equality of crepant pullbacks, without separately assuming that \\(K_U\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 supplies the canonical bundle formula, the threshold interpretation, and attainment. Lemma 1.2 supplies ordinary substitution, effectivity on the specified base, and the exact crepant pullback identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The lc-trivial canonical bundle formula applies to \\( K_X+B_\\omega\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}0=f^*0. \\) For a primitive base valuation \\(\\operatorname{ord}_P\\), resolve the boundary and fibre divisor and write …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, choose a projective connected model \\(f:X\\to T\\), where \\(T\\) is a smooth projective model of \\(K\\), and let \\(B_\\omega\\) be the boundary defined by \\(\\omega\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The lc-trivial canonical bundle formula applies to\n\\[\nK_X+B_\\omega\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}0=f^*0.\n\\]\nFor a primitive base valuation \\(\\operatorname{ord}_P\\), resolve the boundary and fibre divisor and write\n\\[\nK_Z+B_Z=\\mu^*(K_X+B_\\omega),\n\\qquad\n(f\\mu)^*P=\\sum_jm_jE_j.\n\\]\nIf \\(b_j=\\operatorname{coeff}_{E_j}(B_Z)\\), then\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\omega(\\operatorname{ord}_P)\n=\n\\min_j\\frac{1-b_j}{m_j}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Spread \\(G\\), take a projective closure, and normalize. Its Stein factor is finite birational over \\(T\\), because \\(K\\) is algebraically closed in \\(L\\); normality makes it \\(T\\).</p><p>Generic effectivity and generic sub-log-canonicity hold by hypothesis. For the discrepancy-rank condition, resolve the generic pair:\n\\[\nK_{G'}+B_{G'}=\\mu^*(K_G+B_G).\n\\]\nIn the modified discrepancy divisor, omit components having boundary coefficient one. Its rounded divisor is effective and exceptional: all remaining boundary coefficients are less than one, and nonexceptional coefficients belong to \\([0,1]\\). If this rounded divisor is \\(D\\), normality gives \\(\\mu_*\\mathcal O_{G'}(D)=\\mathcal O_G\\). Hence\n\\[\nH^0(G',\\mathcal O_{G'}(D))=K,\n\\]\nwhich verifies rank one. The rational adjoint pullback is already given by the form. The cited lc-trivial canonical bundle formula and b-semiampleness theorem therefore apply.</p><p>Over the DVR of \\(P\\), the threshold is\n\\[\nt_P=\\sup\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:(X,B_\\omega+tf^*P)\n\\text{ is sub-lc over }\\eta_P\\}.\n\\]\nThe resolved horizontal coefficients are at most one, and the vertical coefficients are \\(b_j+tm_j\\). Thus\n\\[\nt_P=\\min_j(1-b_j)/m_j.\n\\]\nAt this value the resolved SNC pair is sub-lc; increasing it violates a minimizing component. Since \\(m_j^{-1}\\operatorname{ord}_{E_j}\\) restricts to \\(\\operatorname{ord}_P\\), this is also the infimum over all prolongations. Homogeneity treats nonprimitive valuations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Replacing the moduli part of \\(\\omega\\) by a sufficiently divisible general member produces the stated ordinary form \\(\\omega_K\\).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, assume \\(\\bar A_\\psi&gt;0\\), and take a common smooth projective base on which the moduli parts descend and the discriminant of \\(\\psi\\) has SNC support.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Replacing the moduli part of \\(\\omega\\) by a sufficiently divisible general member produces the stated ordinary form \\(\\omega_K\\). If an adjoint is pulled back from a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier divisor \\(N\\) on a normal base \\(S\\), this replacement gives\n\\[\nK_S+B'_S=N+\\frac1q\\operatorname{div}_S(g)\n\\]\nand, on a high model \\(\\pi:T\\to S\\),\n\\[\nK_T+B_T+\\frac1qH=\\pi^*(K_S+B'_S).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nK_T+B_{\\omega,T}+M_{\\omega,T}\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}0\n\\]\nand choose \\(q\\) sufficiently divisible with \\(1/q\\le\\gamma\\). Take a general\n\\[\nH\\in|qM_{\\omega,T}|,\n\\]\nor \\(H=0\\) when the system is trivial. It is reduced and transverse to the anchor discriminant strata. Adding \\(H\\) with coefficient one leaves the anchor subpair sub-lc, even when that boundary has negative coefficients. Therefore\n\\[\n0\\le u(H)\\le\\bar A_\\psi(u).\n\\]\nThe boundary \\(B_{\\omega,T}+H/q\\) has discrepancy\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\omega(u)-u(H)/q.\n\\]\nIts adjoint is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-linearly trivial and is represented by a rational pluri-volume form. This gives the asserted inequalities. In particular, for \\(\\omega=\\psi\\) and \\(\\gamma=1/2\\),\n\\[\n\\tfrac12\\bar A_\\psi\\le A_{\\psi_K}\\le\\bar A_\\psi.\n\\]</p><p>For effectivity, at a prime \\(P\\subset U\\), choose a source prime \\(E\\) dominating \\(P\\), of fibre multiplicity \\(k\\ge1\\), and boundary coefficient \\(b_E\\ge0\\). Then\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\omega(\\operatorname{ord}_P)\\le(1-b_E)/k\\le1.\n\\]\nA substituted discrepancy no larger than \\(\\bar A_\\omega\\) consequently has nonnegative boundary coefficient.</p><p>For the pullback assertion, choose representatives satisfying\n\\[\nK_T+B_T+M_T=\\pi^*N,\n\\qquad H=qM_T+\\operatorname{div}(g).\n\\]\nPush forward \\(B_T+H/q\\). Direct substitution gives both displayed equalities, proving \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartierness of the ordinary adjoint and identifying its crepant boundary.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Section 11, Proposition 11.1 and the minimum function (11.5)–(11.6)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After a bounded finite extension of \\(K\\), these data belong to finitely many prepared projective families \\( f:Y\\to B. \\) The base is smooth strict toroidal; the source is strict simplicial toroidal; every source cone …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d,r\\) and \\(\\epsilon_0&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L/K\\) be regular, with \\(\\operatorname{trdeg}_{\\mathbb C}L\\le d\\), and let \\(G/K\\) be a positive-dimensional geometrically \\(\\epsilon_0\\)-lc Fano generic model.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) have index \\(r_0\\le r\\) and effective lc boundary on \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\sigma=\\eta\\otimes\\zeta^{\\otimes r_0},\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\zeta\\) is an absolute volume form on \\(K\\), and \\(\\eta\\) is a relative \\(r_0\\)-pluri-top form.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a bounded finite extension of \\(K\\), these data belong to finitely many prepared projective families\n\\[\nf:Y\\to B.\n\\]\nThe base is smooth strict toroidal; the source is strict simplicial toroidal; every source cone maps onto a base cone. The good parameter open is disjoint from the base boundary. At every allowed field-valued parameter, \\(Y_K\\) maps projectively birationally to \\(G\\), and the rational-map, differential, and form identities specialize.</p><p>The divisor of \\(\\eta\\) as a relative logarithmic pluri-top form is toroidal. Prescribed proper closed subsets may be included in the boundary.</p><p>Let \\(\\varphi\\) be the order function of \\(r_0^{-1}\\operatorname{div}^{\\log}_{Y/B}(\\eta)\\). It is rational linear on each source cone and nonnegative on horizontal rays. The minimum\n\\[\n\\mu(b)=\\min_{f_*x=b}\\varphi(x)\n\\]\nexists, is continuous rational piecewise linear, and may be made linear on every base cone. Let \\(M_0\\) denote the toroidal \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-divisor with order function \\(\\mu\\).</p><p>For another form having effective boundary on \\(G\\), its ratio with \\(\\sigma\\), compared at tensor index \\(p\\), has generic zero and pole divisors on \\(Y_K\\) of combined degree at most \\(Cp\\), where \\(C\\) is uniform.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 gives bounded extensions, embeddings, and presentations. Lemma 2.2 gives the finite prepared families at every allowed parameter. Pullback of polynomial cuts gives the degree bound on their generic fibres. Lemma 2.3 gives \\(M_0\\) and the stated minimum function.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Section 11.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Proposition 2:", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 2:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Bounded finite extensions preserve each barred discrepancy with the original valuation scale.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The geometric Fano varieties admit bounded projective embeddings after a bounded extension of \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The relative reference form has a presentation\n   \\[\n   \\eta=\\frac{P}{Q}(dz_1^0\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dz_n^0)^{\\otimes r_0}\n   \\]\n   with \\(P,Q\\) homogeneous polynomial restrictions of uniformly bounded degree.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. At common tensor index \\(p\\), comparison ratios have polynomial presentations of degree at most \\(Cp\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite extension \\(K'/K\\), regularity makes \\(LK'\\) a field. Fix a prolongation \\(u'\\) with \\(u'|_K=u\\). Every \\(w|_K=u\\) has a compatible prolongation: tensor the completions at \\(w\\) and \\(u'\\) over the completion at \\(u\\), choose a field factor, and keep the old scale. Finite-extension compatibility of form discrepancies gives\n\\[\n\\inf_{w'|_{K'}=u'}A_{\\sigma_{LK'}}(w')\n=\n\\inf_{w|_K=u}A_\\sigma(w).\n\\]\nIf \\(u\\) is integral-valued, multiplying the prolongation by a ramification index at most \\([K':K]\\) restores integrality. An upstairs base form descends by its norm; its normalized divisor is the average of conjugate normalized divisors, and its index is multiplied by a bounded degree.</p><p>BAB bounds the geometric Fano family. Its geometric Picard group is free of bounded rank. Indeed \\(H^1(\\mathcal O)=0\\), bounded Betti ranks bound the Picard rank, and a torsion line bundle \\(T\\) has \\(\\chi(T)=1\\); klt Fano vanishing gives a nonzero section, so its effective numerically trivial divisor is zero. The finite Galois image in this free group has bounded order, since finite subgroups of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_q(\\mathbb Z)\\) inject modulo \\(3\\). After killing that action, the polarization class is invariant. Its projective linear system descends to a Severi–Brauer variety of bounded dimension, which splits over a bounded extension. This gives a bounded embedding.</p><p>The effective form boundary has degree\n\\[\n\\deg_A(-r_0^{-1}\\operatorname{div}_G\\eta)\n=-K_G\\cdot A^{n-1},\n\\]\nbounded by adjunction in the embedded family. A finite linear projection provides \\(z_1^0,\\ldots,z_n^0\\); its differential has bounded poles, hence also bounded zeros by the canonical-degree calculation.</p><p>A rational function with pole divisor of degree at most \\(a\\) is a quotient of polynomial restrictions of degree at most \\(C(1+a)\\): cut each pole prime by a hypersurface of degree at most its degree, multiply with multiplicities, and use normality followed by uniform regularity to lift the numerator. This gives the reference presentation. Comparing effective form boundaries at common tensor index \\(p\\) gives the bound \\(Cp\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Proposition 11.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The bounded presentations of Lemma 2.1 admit finite all-parameter toroidal preparations with the projectivity, cone-surjectivity, strictness, simpliciality, and relative logarithmic form properties stated in Proposition …", "statement": "<p>The bounded presentations of Lemma 2.1 admit finite all-parameter toroidal preparations with the projectivity, cone-surjectivity, strictness, simpliciality, and relative logarithmic form properties stated in Proposition 2.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Hilbert schemes and the coefficients of the reference presentation, retaining the scalar coefficient of the form. On each reduced closure of valid parameter points, shrink so that the fibres are normal and geometrically integral and a generic resolution with its relative form divisor spreads. Fixed SNC coefficients test effectivity and log canonicity. Spread inverse rational maps, their identities, differential identities, and nonzero denominators. Apply the same procedure to the closed complement. Noetherian induction produces finitely many packages and excludes no valid field-valued parameter.</p><p>Apply weak toroidalization with the prescribed closed subsets. For the relative form, induct on relative dimension. Factor through independent relative functions so that the top arrow has relative dimension one; write the determinant form as a top relative factor times a lower factor. Toroidalize the top arrow, marking the divisor of its generic curve form. Every remaining bad component is vertical. Mark its image and the intermediate boundary, and prepare the lower arrow inductively.</p><p>For the normalized main pullback, old boundary equations pull back to monomials times units. In completed toroidal charts, absorb units by unit-valued characters, using formal roots when the finite lattice index requires them; smooth base parameters replace appropriate smooth source parameters. The remaining equations are binomial,\n\\[\nz^{p_j}=(z')^{q_j}.\n\\]\nThe normalized torus branches are obtained by saturation in their character lattices. The cone is the intersection of the product cones with \\(p_*x=q_*y\\). Cone-surjectivity gives the required interior condition. Excellence identifies these completed normal branches with those of the algebraic normalized main component. Rational complementary characters show that the relative logarithmic determinant pulls back unchanged. The marked bad images put the entire relative logarithmic divisor in the boundary.</p><p>Finally take projective toroidal resolutions and projective equidimensionalization. A slicing function, equal to one on primitive rays of the initial regular source cones, remains positive and linear on subdivisions. Apply the cited extension-of-triangulations result without new rays. Since the base is simplicial and source rays map to base rays or zero, each new cone maps onto a base face. This gives a strict simplicial source and a smooth strict base while preserving the form identities. Any newly excluded loci undergo the same noetherian restart before the finite package list is fixed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Section 11.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a prepared family of Proposition 2, every source-cone option over a base cone occurs over every point of the relevant base stratum, with an ordinary-fibre component label.", "statement": "<p>For a prepared family of Proposition 2, every source-cone option over a base cone occurs over every point of the relevant base stratum, with an ordinary-fibre component label. The function\n\\[\n\\mu(b)=\\min_{f_*x=b}\\varphi(x)\n\\]\nexists and is continuous rational piecewise linear.</p>", "proof": "<p>A source stratum maps smoothly with dense open image to the relevant base stratum. Properness makes its closure meet every point. At a deeper point of the closure, the face for the original stratum still maps onto the base cone. In its orbit-closure chart, the boundary equations vanish and the remaining smooth base parameters can be eliminated. The open face stratum is dense in this fibre chart, so actual points with the desired ordinary-fibre label occur.</p><p>On a simplicial source cone, allocate each base coordinate to rays over its axis and choose a ray of least cost. Horizontal rays have nonnegative cost and can be set to zero. There are finitely many cones and choices, giving a rational piecewise linear minimum. Candidates on a face can be approached from incident deeper base cones by specializing the source-stratum closure and adding arbitrarily small new weights. Conversely, a limit of interior minima gives a face candidate after omitting nonnegative horizontal directions. Thus the minima are continuous across faces. Compatible projective subdivisions make \\(\\mu\\) linear on each base cone.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 12.2, Trait comparison", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The labelled rational monomial valuations are compatible algebraic divisorial ray orders.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a prepared family of Proposition 2 and an allowed parameter with field \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u\\ne0\\) be a divisorial valuation of \\(K\\), extended to the proper parameter base.</p><p class=\"li\">After the bounded axis-root preparation, let \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_a\\) be base boundary equations, \\(b_j=u(t_j)&gt;0\\), \\(\\pi\\) a uniformizer of \\(\\mathcal O_u\\), \\(\\tau=u(\\pi)\\), and \\(\\ell=b/\\tau\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}^a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A label is an incident irreducible component of a source stratum in the ordinary geometric parameter fibre.</p><p class=\"li\">For a boundary vector \\(x\\) with \\(f_*x=b\\), let \\(w_x\\) be the labelled monomial valuation, scaled by \\(w_x(\\pi)=\\tau\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For another form \\(\\omega\\) with effective boundary on \\(G\\), compare at common tensor index \\(p\\) and write\n  \\[\n  A_\\omega(v)=A_\\sigma(v)+v(H)/p.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The labelled rational monomial valuations are compatible algebraic divisorial ray orders. For every divisorial \\(w|_K=u\\), with labelled monomial retraction \\(w_x\\),\n\\[\nA_\\sigma(w)=A_\\zeta(u)+\\varphi(x)+E(w),\n\\qquad E(w)\\ge0,\\quad E(w_x)=0.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\sigma(u)=A_\\zeta(u)+\\mu(b).\n\\]\nThere is a uniform \\(C_2\\) such that\n\\[\n|w(H)-w_x(H)|\\le C_2pE(w),\n\\]\nand, along a segment in one labelled slice cell, including incident endpoints,\n\\[\n|w_x(H)-w_{x'}(H)|\\le C_2p\\|x-x'\\|.\n\\]\nThe discrepancy identities do not require absolute log canonicity of \\(\\sigma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2 construct the actual labelled algebraic ray valuations. Lemma 3.3 proves the excess and minimum identities. Lemma 3.4 proves both uniform comparison estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Section 12.1, formula (TC)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After taking roots of the base axes of one bounded order and normalizing, every mapped face-span lattice maps surjectively onto its base-face lattice.", "statement": "<p>After taking roots of the base axes of one bounded order and normalizing, every mapped face-span lattice maps surjectively onto its base-face lattice.</p><p>For a source cone \\(\\delta\\), with span lattice \\(N_\\delta\\), the actual normalized main trait chart has cone and lattice\n\\[\nD_\\delta=\\{(x,e)\\in\\delta\\times\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}:f_*x=\\ell e\\},\n\\qquad\n\\Lambda_\\delta=\\{(n,e)\\in N_\\delta\\oplus\\mathbb Z:f_*n=\\ell e\\}.\n\\]\nIts monomial valuation satisfies\n\\[\n(mx,m\\tau)\\in\\Lambda_\\delta\n\\Longrightarrow mw_x\\text{ is integral-valued}.\n\\]\nEvaluation of a rational function is continuous on the entire closed slice.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the root order divisible by the finitely many image-lattice indices. Replacing the base lattice by \\(h\\mathbb Z^a\\) and each source lattice by its preimage makes every relevant map onto, including on faces. Monoid saturation gives the new charts; the relative logarithmic determinant is unchanged in rational character spaces.</p><p>Write \\(M=N_\\delta^\\vee\\), \\(P=\\delta^\\vee\\cap M\\), and let \\(p_j\\) be the pullback characters of the base axes. Integral surjectivity splits\n\\[\nM=f^*\\mathbb Z^a\\oplus M_{\\mathrm{rel}}.\n\\]\nThus prescribed unit factors on the \\(p_j\\) extend to a unit-valued character using integral powers, without extracting residue roots. The resulting completed-ring substitution is an automorphism, also for a singular toric chart. Eliminate smooth base parameters and absorb the remaining trait units. The equations become\n\\[\nX^{p_j}=\\pi^{\\ell_j}.\n\\]</p><p>The character pushout\n\\[\n(M\\oplus\\mathbb Z)/\\langle(f^*c,-\\ell\\cdot c)\\rangle\n\\]\nis \\(M_{\\mathrm{rel}}\\oplus\\mathbb Z\\), hence torsion-free. The image monoid is sharp because a relative-interior lift of \\(\\ell\\) gives a strictly positive functional. Its saturation gives one normalized main torus branch. Excellence, flat completion, and nonzerodivisibility of boundary monomials identify this with the actual algebraic main branch.</p><p>Minimum weights of monoid series define valuations. A monomial ideal is finitely generated, so finitely many support exponents determine each minimum on the closed cone. This proves continuity, including faces, and the integral-lattice implication.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Section 12.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The ray orders of Lemma 3.1 are intrinsically determined by their weights and their ordinary-fibre component labels.", "statement": "<p>The ray orders of Lemma 3.1 are intrinsically determined by their weights and their ordinary-fibre component labels. They agree at incident charts and restrict compatibly after adjoining a root of the uniformizer.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a face \\(\\beta\\le\\delta\\) mapping onto the base cone, saturated face lattices give\n\\[\n\\Lambda_\\delta/\\Lambda_\\beta\\simeq N_\\delta/N_\\beta.\n\\]\nIndeed \\(f_*N_\\beta=\\mathbb Z^a\\) supplies the necessary correction to any class in \\(N_\\delta/N_\\beta\\). The quotient cones agree as well. Hence the face centre has the original integral orbit-closure lattice and no hidden finite orbit cover. Its ordinary-fibre branch is the specified label.</p><p>Use effective Cartier multiples of boundary primes. Compare powers of their equations with powers of \\(\\pi\\) of equal prescribed weight. The normalized blowups of these two-generated ideals impose the equal-weight walls and extract the desired ray. In the completed chart these are toric subdivisions. The ray prime remains irreducible: its orbit-lattice map has torsion-free kernel and geometrically integral generic fibre over the supporting orbit closure. Flat completion introduces no additional component.</p><p>The scaled order of this prime is the minimum-weight order on monoid series, as seen from monomial cutoff ideals on a smooth toric refinement. Contracting the completed ray prime gives a height-one algebraic prime. The primitive unit splitting and the quotient-lattice identity give uniqueness over the labelled component, so orders agree at all incident charts.</p><p>If \\(\\pi=\\rho^d\\), the lattice map is\n\\[\n(n,e)\\longmapsto(n,de).\n\\]\nThe weight \\((x,\\tau/d)\\) restricts to \\((x,\\tau)\\). The quotient calculation is independent of \\(\\ell\\), so the ordinary-fibre label is preserved.</p><p>The preliminary saturation is essential. The paper’s example\n\\[\nt=x^2(y^2+x)\n\\]\non \\(y(y^2+x)\\ne0\\) acquires, after \\(t=\\pi^2\\), two primes \\(q=y\\) and \\(q=-y\\), where \\(q=\\pi/x\\). They have the same old boundary weights but differ on \\(q-y\\). The Kummer normalization separates their labels.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Sections 12.3 and Proposition 12.2, formulas (T) and (TE)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 3, there are finitely many relative logarithmic coordinate lists \\(z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)\\), of bounded rational presentation degree, such that \\(w_x\\) is Gauss on \\(K(z)\\), and …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 3, there are finitely many relative logarithmic coordinate lists \\(z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)\\), of bounded rational presentation degree, such that \\(w_x\\) is Gauss on \\(K(z)\\), and\n\\[\nF=\\frac{\\eta}{(d\\log z_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\log z_n)^{\\otimes r_0}}\n\\]\nis toroidal of order function \\(r_0\\varphi\\).</p><p>For every \\(w|_K=u\\),\n\\[\nA_\\sigma(w)=A_\\zeta(u)+\\varphi(x)+E(w),\n\\qquad E(w)\\ge0,\n\\]\nwith equality \\(E(w_x)=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take characters rationally complementary to the base axes and regular units with independent relative stratum differentials. Their character block and stratum-coordinate block are invertible. Finitely many neighbourhoods suffice, and their functions have bounded rational presentations. The retained identities specialize even at nondominant parameter maps.</p><p>For rational interior weights, complementary characters and \\(\\pi\\) have independent angular directions. The free stratum units supply the remaining independent residues. Thus \\(w_x\\) is Gauss. Continuity extends the formula to faces and real weights. Rational \\(w_x\\) is divisorial by discreteness and the relative Abhyankar equality.</p><p>A divisorial \\(w\\) can be followed on the geometric completed trait with its scale unchanged; its centre supplies the label. These completed fields calculate charts and function values, not absolute discrepancies.</p><p>For the discrepancy calculation, take a finite root uniformizer clearing the ratios \\(w(z_i)/u(\\pi)\\), and put \\(z_i'=\\widetilde\\pi^{-h_i}z_i\\). On the rational field in the \\(z_i'\\), use the smooth unit torus over an extracted base prime. Let \\(T\\) be this model and \\(P_0\\) the reduced fibre. Finite-extension compatibility, the form ratio \\(F\\), and invariance of relative logarithmic differentials under base rescaling give\n\\[\nE(w)=A_{T,P_0}(w|_{K(z')}).\n\\]\nThe smooth reduced pair is lc. For \\(w_x\\), the Gauss rule is the scaled order of \\(P_0\\), so the excess is zero. Every cone option occurs and the rational minimum has a label. Minimizing proves\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\sigma(u)=A_\\zeta(u)+\\mu(b).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Proposition 12.2, formula (TV)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every comparison function \\(H\\) of Proposition 3, \\( |w(H)-w_x(H)|\\le C_2pE(w), \\qquad |w_x(H)-w_{x'}(H)|\\le C_2p\\|x-x'\\| \\) with a uniform constant, in the stated retraction and labelled-segment settings.", "statement": "<p>For every comparison function \\(H\\) of Proposition 3,\n\\[\n|w(H)-w_x(H)|\\le C_2pE(w),\n\\qquad\n|w_x(H)-w_{x'}(H)|\\le C_2p\\|x-x'\\|\n\\]\nwith a uniform constant, in the stated retraction and labelled-segment settings.</p>", "proof": "<p>The generic zero and pole divisors of \\(H\\), counted with multiplicities, have combined degree at most \\(C_1p\\).</p><p>For retraction, take a root uniformizer large enough that the new ray has primitive vector\n\\[\nv_0=(dx/\\tau,1).\n\\]\nIts last coordinate splits the lattice, so the open ray chart is a torus over a DVR with one reduced smooth vertical divisor. Follow \\(w\\) on the algebraic cut-ideal modification. Equal-cut ratios are units, placing its centre in this regular open ray locus. Regularity and the reduced smooth fibre descend from the geometric completed chart by regular faithfully flat base change.</p><p>Factor the local principal divisor of \\(H\\) into its vertical order and horizontal prime factors \\(D\\). The vertical order is \\(w_x(H)\\). For each horizontal factor, choose a nonzero polynomial \\(q(z)\\) vanishing on its generic image with\n\\[\n\\deg q\\le C_3\\deg D.\n\\]\nBounded presentations and Bézout give this degree estimate. Normalize its coefficients in the rescaled coordinates so that the reduction is nonzero. Then\n\\[\n0\\le w(D)\\le w(q).\n\\]\nA polynomial of degree \\(d_q\\) on a smooth torus has threshold at least \\(1/d_q\\): cut by successive general affine linear sections to a line, use the vanishing-order bound there, and apply smooth-divisor inversion of adjunction back up. Inversion of adjunction along the reduced fibre gives\n\\[\nw(q)\\le(\\deg q)E(w).\n\\]\nSumming with zero and pole multiplicities proves the first estimate. The auxiliary root degree does not enter the generic degree budget.</p><p>For variation, a graph-image equation gives\n\\[\n\\sum_j a_j(z)H^j=0,\n\\qquad \\deg_z a_j\\le C_4p.\n\\]\nBézout bounds this degree using \\(n-1\\) bounded \\(z\\)-cuts and one \\(H\\)-cut of degree at most \\(C_1p\\). The Gauss values of the \\(a_j\\) are finite minima of affine functions with Lipschitz constant \\(C_5p\\). The value \\(w_x(H)\\) is among\n\\[\n\\frac{w_x(a_i)-w_x(a_j)}{j-i},\\qquad i\\ne j.\n\\]\nIt is continuous on the labelled closed segment by Lemma 3.1. Partition at the finitely many affine breakpoints and candidate intersections. On each remaining interval it follows one affine candidate. Adding lengths proves the second estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 13.1 and Proposition 13.2, Effective dlt model and lc incidence", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a smooth projective toroidal resolution \\(P\\to Y\\) and a finite ordinary \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial dlt MMP over \\(B\\) ending at an effective dlt pair \\((P_j,D_j)\\), crepant to \\((Y,C^\\natural)\\), with …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:Y\\to B\\) be one of the algebraic prepared parameter families, including its local bounded Kummer changes, over an algebraically closed residue field.</p><p class=\"li\">The base is smooth, the morphism is projective with connected fibres, and its generic fibre is geometrically integral and birational to the effective lc form model.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  g(x)=\\varphi(x)-\\mu(f_*x)\\ge0,\n  \\]\n  linear on each source cone, and\n  \\[\n  C^\\natural\n  =\\partial Y-r_0^{-1}\\operatorname{div}^{\\log}_{Y/B}(\\eta)+f^*M_0.\n  \\]\n  Then \\(K_Y+C^\\natural\\sim_{\\mathbb Q,B}0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth projective toroidal resolution \\(P\\to Y\\) and a finite ordinary \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial dlt MMP over \\(B\\) ending at an effective dlt pair \\((P_j,D_j)\\), crepant to \\((Y,C^\\natural)\\), with\n\\[\nK_{P_j}+D_j\\sim_{\\mathbb Q,B}0.\n\\]\nIts lc centres correspond to those of the crepant subpair on \\(P\\), preserving inclusions and proper images in \\(B\\).</p><p>Furthermore, let \\(s\\) have nonzero base cone \\(\\tau_s\\). Let \\(\\delta_0\\) be a source face on which \\(g=0\\), let \\(V=\\overline{O_{\\delta_0}}\\), allowing \\(\\delta_0=0\\) and \\(V=Y\\), and let \\(C_s\\) be any connected component of \\(V_s\\). There is a source cone \\(\\beta\\) such that\n\\[\ng|_\\beta=0,\\qquad f_*(\\beta)=\\tau_s,\\qquad\nO_\\beta\\subset V,\\qquad O_\\beta\\cap C_s\\ne\\varnothing.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The nonnegative ray discrepancies \\(g\\) make \\((Y,C^\\natural)\\) sub-lc. Lemmas 4.1–4.2 give the finite effective dlt crepant model and centre correspondence, proving the first assertion.</p><p>For incidence, a rational minimum over a vector in \\(\\operatorname{relint}\\tau_s\\) gives an all-zero cone mapping onto \\(\\tau_s\\). Its stratum occurs over \\(s\\) by the ordinary-fibre chart calculation. This handles \\(V=Y\\).</p><p>For \\(V\\ne Y\\), separate the chosen connected component of \\(V_s\\) by a pointed étale neighbourhood. The finite Stein algebra splits over the henselization; its idempotents descend to such a neighbourhood. Normality makes the resulting irreducible components disjoint. Thus \\(C_s\\) becomes exactly the pointed fibre of one closed stratum \\(V_2\\).</p><p>Apply the first assertion on this neighbourhood. One lc centre maps onto \\(V_2\\); another maps onto the closure of an all-zero stratum over \\(\\tau_s\\). Inside the latter choose an inclusion-minimal lc centre whose image contains \\(s\\). Kollár’s linking theorem applies to the proper connected effective dlt log Calabi–Yau fibration. It gives a subcentre of the first centre, containing \\(s\\) in its image, which is \\(\\mathbb P^1\\)-linked to that minimal centre. Linked centres have the same base image, contained in \\(\\overline{O_{\\tau_s}}\\).</p><p>Return the centre inclusions through the finite MMP and the toroidal resolution. They give an all-zero closed stratum inside \\(V_2\\), with base image containing \\(s\\) and contained in \\(\\overline{O_{\\tau_s}}\\). Its image is a base-stratum closure, hence equals \\(\\overline{O_{\\tau_s}}\\). Its cone maps onto \\(\\tau_s\\). At a fibre closure point, eliminate the remaining smooth base parameters; the open stratum is dense in that branch and meets \\(C_s\\). Projecting through the pointed étale neighbourhood proves the assertion on the original fibre.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 13.1, scaling construction, limiting threshold, centre preservation, and movable representatives", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a dlt MMP with scaling for \\(K_P+D\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(P\\to Y\\) be a smooth strict toroidal resolution, \\(C_P^\\natural\\) the crepant boundary, and\n\\[\nD=\\max\\{C_P^\\natural,0\\},\\qquad N=D-C_P^\\natural.\n\\]\nThen \\((P,D)\\) is effective dlt, \\(N\\ge0\\), and\n\\[\nK_P+D\\sim_{\\mathbb Q,B}N,\\qquad\nA_{C^\\natural}(v)=A_D(v)+v(N).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a dlt MMP with scaling for \\(K_P+D\\). The lc places and their centre incidences are preserved. If infinite, its scaling thresholds tend to zero. On a fixed model \\(P_j\\) after the last divisorial contraction, or on a finite nef endpoint, for every rational \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), relatively ample Cartier \\(A\\), and finite list of primes, there is an effective representative avoiding the list with\n\\[\nE_\\delta\\sim_{\\mathbb Q,B}N_j+\\delta A.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The lc places agree because lc centres of the SNC pair \\((P,D)\\) are intersections of coefficient-one components and are not contained in truncated negative components. Thus their orders of \\(N\\) vanish.</p><p>Choose ample \\(H\\) with \\(K_P+D+H\\) nef over \\(B\\). For rational \\(t&gt;0\\), choose small \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) and a general small-coefficient representative\n\\[\nT_{t,\\epsilon}\\sim_{\\mathbb Q,B}tH+\\epsilon D.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\Delta_t=(1-\\epsilon)D+T_{t,\\epsilon}\n\\sim_{\\mathbb Q,B}D+tH\n\\]\nis big and klt. Its transforms remain klt while the preceding thresholds are at least \\(t\\). Klt cone, contraction, flip, and basepoint-free theorems therefore construct the rational scaling thresholds and ordinary dlt steps. Crepancy of \\(C^\\natural\\) gives, on each common resolution,\n\\[\na^*N_i-(a^+)^*N_{i+1}\\ge0.\n\\]\nThus \\(N_i\\ge0\\), and a Mori fibre ending is impossible.</p><p>If the thresholds had positive limit, work after the last divisorial contraction. Klt representatives of \\(D_j+tH_j\\) on a compact positive interval can be put in a rational polytope with fixed ample part and small ample spanning directions. Their adjoints are big. BCHM gives finitely many marked ample models. Every late model is one: perturb the nef scaling adjoint in the spanning directions to make it ample. Small \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial modifications identify numerical divisor spaces by negativity and preserve sections. No marked pair can recur, because each genuinely negative step has a nonzero effective pullback difference. Hence the thresholds tend to zero.</p><p>For every lc place,\n\\[\n0\\le A_{D_i}(v)\\le A_{C^\\natural}(v),\n\\qquad\nA_{C^\\natural}(v)=A_{D_i}(v)+v(N_i).\n\\]\nThe lc places remain exactly the original ones, and their centres avoid \\(\\operatorname{Supp}N_i\\). All negative curves lie in that support. The contraction and flip are isomorphisms near each centre’s generic point. Test an inclusion at the smaller centre’s generic point; this preserves inclusions in both directions and, by properness, base images.</p><p>For movable representatives, in the finite nef case \\(N_j+\\delta A\\) is ample. In the infinite case choose \\(i\\) so late that \\(\\delta A-\\lambda_iH_j\\) is ample. Fix an ample \\(A_i^+\\) on \\(P_i\\), then choose \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) so that\n\\[\n\\delta A-\\lambda_iH_j-\\alpha(A_i^+)_{P_j}\n\\]\nis ample. Add general representatives of this divisor and of\n\\[\nN_i+\\lambda_iH_i+\\alpha A_i^+,\n\\]\ntransforming the latter back across the small map. Both can avoid the prescribed primes on the affine base, and their sum gives \\(E_\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 13.1, vanishing of the exceptional error", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the fixed finite model \\(P_j\\) of Lemma 4.1, the effective divisor \\(N_j\\) is zero.", "statement": "<p>For the fixed finite model \\(P_j\\) of Lemma 4.1, the effective divisor \\(N_j\\) is zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>First \\(N_j\\) is vertical. On a common generic-fibre resolution of \\(P,P_j\\), and the original effective model \\(G_{\\mathrm{gen}}\\), the pullback of \\(N_j\\) is bounded by that of \\(N\\). The latter is exceptional over \\(G_{\\mathrm{gen}}\\), so \\(N_j\\) has zero trace there.</p><p>Suppose a horizontal component \\(F\\) has coefficient \\(n_F&gt;0\\). Choose \\(E_\\delta\\) avoiding \\(F\\), and on the generic fibre write\n\\[\nE_\\delta=N_j+\\delta A+m_\\delta^{-1}\\operatorname{div}(h_\\delta).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nm_\\delta^{-1}\\operatorname{ord}_F(h_\\delta)\n=-n_F-\\delta\\operatorname{ord}_F(A).\n\\]\nTaking trace on \\(G_{\\mathrm{gen}}\\) gives\n\\[\nm_\\delta^{-1}\\operatorname{div}(h_\\delta)+\\delta A_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\ge0.\n\\]\nChoose a Cartier majorant \\(H_G\\ge A_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\), using a sufficiently positive twist of the coherent divisorial sheaf. Pulling back the resulting effective \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier divisor to \\(F\\) yields\n\\[\n0&lt;n_F\\le\n\\delta\\bigl(\\operatorname{ord}_F(H_G)-\\operatorname{ord}_F(A)\\bigr),\n\\]\nimpossible for arbitrarily small \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>Next \\(N_j\\) is very exceptional. Over a boundary prime \\(T\\subset B\\), a rational minimum over its axis gives a zero-gap toroidal lc centre dominating \\(T\\). It persists on \\(P_j\\) and avoids \\(N_j\\). A component of the Cartier pullback of \\(T\\) containing that centre dominates \\(T\\) and is absent from \\(N_j\\). Over a nonboundary prime, no component of \\(N_j\\) dominates it, and surjectivity supplies a dominating prime.</p><p>Choose \\(\\delta_k\\downarrow0\\) and effective representatives avoiding every component of \\(N_j\\). Their restrictions show that \\(N_j\\) is nef on the required very general curves of each component over \\(B\\). Apply the cited very-exceptional negativity lemma to\n\\[\nD'=-N_j,\\qquad (D')^+=0,\\quad (D')^-=N_j.\n\\]\nIt gives \\(D'\\ge0\\), hence \\(N_j=0\\). This occurs on the fixed finite model and precludes further negative steps.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Theorem 10.1, Bounded-fibre estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a form \\(\\sigma_K\\), of index bounded only by \\(d,\\epsilon_0,r\\), such that \\( \\tfrac12\\bar A_\\sigma\\le A_{\\sigma_K}\\le\\bar A_\\sigma. \\) If \\(\\psi\\) has effective boundary on \\(G\\), \\(A_\\psi>0\\) on nonzero …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L/K\\) be regular, with \\(\\operatorname{trdeg}_{\\mathbb C}L\\le d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G/K\\) be a positive-dimensional geometrically \\(\\epsilon_0\\)-lc Fano projective model, where \\(\\epsilon_0&gt;0\\) is fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) have index at most \\(r\\), effective boundary on \\(G\\), and \\(A_\\sigma\\ge0\\) on all absolute divisorial valuations.</p><p class=\"li\">For nonzero divisorial \\(u\\) of \\(K\\), use\n  \\[\n  \\bar A_\\omega(u)=\\inf_{w|_K=u}A_\\omega(w).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Integral-valued valuations need not be primitive.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. There is a form \\(\\sigma_K\\), of index bounded only by \\(d,\\epsilon_0,r\\), such that\n   \\[\n   \\tfrac12\\bar A_\\sigma\\le A_{\\sigma_K}\\le\\bar A_\\sigma.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(\\psi\\) has effective boundary on \\(G\\), \\(A_\\psi&gt;0\\) on nonzero absolute divisorial valuations, and \\(A_\\sigma\\le CA_\\psi\\), then for every \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\) there is a uniform \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) such that every nonzero integral-valued \\(u\\) with \\(\\bar A_\\psi(u)&lt;\\delta\\) has an integral-valued divisorial lift \\(v\\) satisfying\n   \\[\n   v|_K\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}u,\\qquad A_\\psi(v)&lt;\\lambda.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Let \\(\\chi,\\psi\\) have effective boundaries on \\(G\\), without requiring absolute lc in this part. If\n   \\[\n   A_\\chi(w)\\ge cA_\\psi(w)\n   \\quad\\text{whenever }w\\ne0,\\ A_\\sigma(w)=0,\n   \\]\n   then there is \\(D\\ge0\\), depending only on \\(d,\\epsilon_0,r,c\\), such that\n   \\[\n   A_\\chi(w)\\ge cA_\\psi(w)-DA_\\sigma(w)\n   \\]\n   whenever \\(w|_K=0\\), or \\(u=w|_K\\ne0\\) and \\(\\bar A_\\sigma(u)=0\\).</p><p>If \\(K=\\mathbb C\\), part 1 is vacuous and a constant index-one form suffices; part 2 is vacuous.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1, 5.2, and 5.3 prove parts 1, 2, and 3 respectively.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Section 12.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 5, part 1 holds.", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 5, part 1 holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the prepared families before the axis extensions. Proposition 3 and bounded-extension compatibility give\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\sigma(u)=A_\\zeta(u)+u(M_{0,K})\n\\]\nfor every allowed parameter, including a nondominant one.</p><p>Apply the canonical bundle formula of Proposition 1 to the universal generic form, using a projective model carrying the original effective lc generic boundary. The minimum identity identifies the moduli divisor with \\(M_0\\). Hence \\(M_0\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-semiample. A base-free multiple on a higher model descends by the projection formula and \\(\\pi_*\\mathcal O=\\mathcal O\\). Finitely many packages give one uniform \\(m\\ge2\\) with \\(mM_0\\) globally generated.</p><p>Resolve the actual parameter map and choose a general\n\\[\nH=mM_{0,K}+\\operatorname{div}(h)\n\\]\ntransverse to a log resolution of the lc form boundary. Along \\(H\\) the old boundary coefficient is zero, so adding the reduced divisor preserves lc:\n\\[\nu(H)\\le\\bar A_\\sigma(u).\n\\]\nThe form\n\\[\n\\sigma_K=\\zeta^{\\otimes m}/h\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\nA_{\\sigma_K}(u)=\\bar A_\\sigma(u)-u(H)/m.\n\\]\nThis lies between \\(\\bar A_\\sigma/2\\) and \\(\\bar A_\\sigma\\). Descend by norm over any bounded preliminary extension.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Section 12.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 5, part 2 holds with uniform choices of \\(\\delta\\).", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 5, part 2 holds with uniform choices of \\(\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a rational divisorial \\(w|_K=u\\) with \\(A_\\psi(w)\\) small. Make only the bounded parameter and axis extensions, preserving scale. Let \\(D_0\\) clear their bounded ramification denominators, so \\(b,\\tau\\in D_0^{-1}\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p>The minimum and excess formulas give\n\\[\nA_\\sigma(w)=\\bar A_\\sigma(u)+\\varphi(x)-\\mu(b)+E(w).\n\\]\nAll three terms are nonnegative, so \\(E(w)\\le CA_\\psi(w)\\). Proposition 3 gives\n\\[\nA_\\psi(w_x)\\le C_6A_\\psi(w),\\qquad C_6=1+CC_2,\n\\]\nand a uniform Lipschitz constant \\(L_0\\) for \\(A_\\psi(w_x)\\) on each closed labelled cell.</p><p>Write \\(x=\\sum_{i=1}^sd_ir_i\\) in primitive rays. Uniformly bound \\(s\\le S_0\\), \\(\\sum\\|f_*r_i\\|_\\infty\\le P'_0\\), and \\(\\sum\\|r_i\\|\\le R_0\\). Choose \\(Q&gt;P'_0\\), \\(Q\\ge2\\), with \\(Q^{-1}\\le\\epsilon\\). Pigeonhole the \\(Q^s+1\\) points\n\\[\nj(D_0d_1,\\ldots,D_0d_s)\\pmod{\\mathbb Z^s}.\n\\]\nTheir difference gives \\(m=D_0h\\), \\(1\\le h\\le Q^s\\), and nonnegative integers \\(q_i\\) satisfying\n\\[\n|md_i-q_i|&lt;Q^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe integral residual \\(\\sum q_if_*r_i-mb\\) has norm less than one, hence is zero. Thus\n\\[\ny'=\\sum(q_i/m)r_i,\\qquad f_*y'=b,\\qquad\nm\\|y'-x\\|&lt;R_0\\epsilon.\n\\]\nUse the incident label even when some \\(q_i=0\\).</p><p>Now \\((my',m\\tau)\\) is integral in the trait lattice. Hence \\(v=mw_{y'}\\) is integral-valued and divisorial, and restricts to \\(mu\\). Moreover\n\\[\nA_\\psi(v)\\le mC_6A_\\psi(w)+L_0R_0\\epsilon.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\epsilon\\) first so that the last term is less than \\(\\lambda/2\\), then choose the input threshold less than\n\\[\n\\lambda/(2D_0Q^{S_0}C_6).\n\\]\nThe infimum defining \\(\\bar A_\\psi\\) supplies \\(w\\), and the desired estimate follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Sections 13.3–13.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 5, part 3 holds.", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 5, part 3 holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>First suppose \\(u=w|_K\\ne0\\) and \\(\\bar A_\\sigma(u)=0\\). Then\n\\[\nA_\\sigma(w)=g(x)+E(w).\n\\]\nWrite \\(h=g(x)\\). Retain the ray coefficients where \\(g=0\\), setting the others to zero, to obtain \\(x_0\\). Finiteness of the ray data gives\n\\[\n\\|x-x_0\\|\\le C_0h,\\qquad\nb-f_*x_0\\ge0,\\qquad\n\\|b-f_*x_0\\|\\le C_0h.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(h=0\\), use \\(w_*=w_x\\). If the base cone is zero, the segment from \\(x\\) to \\(x_0\\) remains in the same slice. Otherwise let \\(V\\) be the closed zero-face stratum and \\(C_s\\) the connected component containing the label of \\(x\\). Proposition 4 gives an all-zero cone over the base cone meeting \\(C_s\\).</p><p>Partition \\(C_s\\) by irreducible components of ordinary stratum fibres. Their number is uniformly bounded by Bézout in the fixed finite families. Their incidence graph is connected: a partition into disconnected graph components would give disjoint nonempty closed subsets of \\(C_s\\). A simple path therefore uses a uniformly bounded number of edges. At each edge, a deeper incidence point gives one chart with the required face label.</p><p>Transport \\(x_0\\) through these cones. Cone-surjectivity supplies lifts of \\(b-f_*x_0\\) of size at most \\(Ch\\). Interpolate slightly with rational relative-interior lifts to obtain rational points in the respective cones, still within \\(Ch\\) of \\(x_0\\). Join consecutive points inside their larger incident cones. The total labelled path length is at most \\(C_2h\\). Its endpoint gives a divisorial \\(w_*\\) with \\(A_\\sigma(w_*)=0\\).</p><p>For \\(\\omega=\\chi,\\psi\\), write\n\\[\nA_\\omega=A_\\sigma+q_\\omega,\\qquad q_\\omega(v)=v(H_\\omega)/p_\\omega.\n\\]\nRetraction and path variation from Proposition 3 give\n\\[\n|q_\\omega(w)-q_\\omega(w_*)|\n\\le C_3(E(w)+h)=C_3A_\\sigma(w).\n\\]\nThe assumed inequality at \\(w_*\\) gives\n\\[\nA_\\chi(w)-cA_\\psi(w)\n\\ge-\\bigl(|1-c|+(1+c)C_3\\bigr)A_\\sigma(w).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(w|_K=0\\), adjoin \\(q\\) to both fields and extend each form by wedging every tensor factor with \\(d\\log q\\). For every divisorial \\(v\\) of \\(L(q)\\),\n\\[\nA_{\\omega^{\\mathrm{ext}}}(v)\n=A_\\omega(v|_L)+J(v),\\qquad J(v)\\ge0,\n\\]\nwith the same \\(J\\) for all forms. On a smooth product model, \\(J\\) is the discrepancy for the reduced pullback of the extracted prime and the two sections \\(q=0,\\infty\\); the pair is SNC. Thus the extended reference is lc and the zero-set hypothesis persists.</p><p>The barred extended reference discrepancy at \\(\\operatorname{ord}_{q=0}\\) of \\(K(q)\\) is zero. Extend \\(w\\) by the Gauss valuation assigning \\(q\\) weight one. It is rational divisorial, restricts to \\(\\operatorname{ord}_{q=0}\\), and has \\(J=0\\). Apply the case already proved. The dimension has increased by only one, preserving uniform dependence on the original bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 10.3, Geometric tower step", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a projective Mori contraction \\(H\\to U\\) over \\(Q\\), with \\(Q(H)=P\\), and \\(R=Q(U)\\), such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P/Q\\) be a regular extension of positive transcendence degree, with a projective Fano type model over \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Finitely many forms on \\(P\\) have effective boundaries on this model.</p><p class=\"li\">A variety is of Fano type if it has an effective klt boundary with ample negative adjoint.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a projective Mori contraction \\(H\\to U\\) over \\(Q\\), with \\(Q(H)=P\\), and \\(R=Q(U)\\), such that:\n\\[\nP/R,\\ R/Q\\text{ are regular},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{trdeg}_Q R&lt;\\operatorname{trdeg}_Q P.\n\\]\nIts generic fibre is positive-dimensional and geometrically \\(1/10\\)-lc Fano. Both \\(H\\) and \\(U\\) are of Fano type over \\(Q\\). All carried form boundaries remain effective on \\(H\\), and substitutions whose discrepancies are no larger than the relevant barred discrepancies have effective traces on \\(U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 6.1 with \\(\\epsilon_0=1/10\\), then run a \\(K\\)-MMP over \\(Q\\). The canonical class is not pseudo-effective because the variety is of Fano type. The klt Mori fibre theorem gives \\(H\\to U\\). Underlying discrepancies improve, so \\(H\\) remains geometrically \\(1/10\\)-lc; non-extraction preserves effective carried boundaries.</p><p>The Fano type structure on the extraction supplies an effective klt log Calabi–Yau boundary which is big. Push it through the MMP. Its adjoint is crepant by negativity, and its boundary remains big by pushforward of sections. A big-boundary perturbation makes \\(H\\) of Fano type. The generic fibre has ample negative canonical class and inherits geometric \\(1/10\\)-log-canonicity. Connectedness gives regularity of \\(P/R\\), and \\(R/Q\\) is regular as a subextension of \\(P/Q\\). Positive relative dimension lowers transcendence degree.</p><p>To show \\(U\\) is of Fano type, choose \\(B_H\\) with\n\\[\nA_H=-(K_H+B_H)\n\\]\nample and fix ample \\(A_U\\). For small rational \\(e&gt;0\\), choose a small-coefficient general effective\n\\[\nD_H\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}A_H-eh^*A_U\n\\]\nso that \\((H,B_H+D_H)\\) is klt. Then\n\\[\nK_H+B_H+D_H\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}-eh^*A_U.\n\\]\nProposition 1 applies. Its threshold is positive by attainment and klt. Replace the semiample moduli part by a sufficiently divisible general member; the resulting effective base boundary has discrepancy between one half and all of the threshold discrepancy. The exact pullback identity gives a klt pair \\((U,B_U)\\) with\n\\[\nK_U+B_U\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}-eA_U.\n\\]\nAfter a finite Galois extension, average the conjugate boundaries to descend this structure to \\(Q\\). Finally Proposition 1 gives effectivity of all stated substitutions on \\(U\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 10.3, extraction construction; also used in Section 9.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They admit a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial extraction carrying the effective crepant klt boundary.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(X\\) carry an effective klt pair, and first take a small \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorialization. For any fixed \\(\\epsilon_0&lt;1\\), the geometric prime valuations with underlying log discrepancy below \\(\\epsilon_0\\) form a finite Galois-invariant set.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They admit a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial extraction carrying the effective crepant klt boundary. The new underlying variety is geometrically \\(\\epsilon_0\\)-lc. Any carried effective form boundary remains effective. If the initial pair is log Fano, the extraction is of Fano type.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a geometric log resolution, with reduced full SNC support \\(D=\\sum D_i\\), write\n\\[\nA_X(v)=A_{V,D}(v)+\\sum_i a_iv(D_i),\\qquad a_i&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor a primitive prime valuation with discrepancy below one, the first summand is a nonnegative integer and must vanish. Thus it is a monomial lc place of the reduced SNC pair, and the positive coefficients bound its integral weights. Finitely many strata and weights give finiteness.</p><p>Resolve all desired divisors. Keep their crepant boundary coefficients, which lie in \\((0,1)\\), and the strict boundary. At every other exceptional divisor choose a coefficient below one but above zero and its crepant coefficient. The resulting klt adjoint differs from the old pullback by an effective exceptional divisor supported away from the desired primes. Run the relative big-boundary klt MMP. The desired divisors are untouched generically; at a nef endpoint negativity eliminates the exceptional error. This extracts precisely the chosen set.</p><p>For underlying varieties,\n\\[\nK_{X'}+B_{\\mathrm{exc}}=\\pi^*K_X,\\qquad B_{\\mathrm{exc}}\\ge0.\n\\]\nNonextracted discrepancies improve, and extracted primes have underlying discrepancy one on \\(X'\\). A carried effective boundary has discrepancy at most the old underlying discrepancy at each extracted prime, so remains effective.</p><p>A log Fano negative adjoint pulls back to a nef and big divisor. A Kodaira decomposition and a sufficiently small effective perturbation make it ample while preserving klt. Thus the extraction remains of Fano type. Galois-invariant resolutions and extractions, or their relative spread, give the construction over \\(Q\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 9.1, Lifting and Mixing", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Lifting.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix positive integers \\(d,r\\) and \\(C&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All fields are finitely generated over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All inequalities are homogeneous on nonzero divisorial valuations and allow positive rational scalings.</p><p class=\"li\">Integral-valued valuations need not be primitive.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Lifting. Let \\(P/Q\\) be regular, \\(\\operatorname{trdeg}_{\\mathbb C}P\\le d\\), with a projective Fano type generic model. Let \\(\\sigma,\\psi\\) have effective boundaries there, \\(\\sigma\\) have index at most \\(r\\), and\n\\[\nA_\\psi&gt;0,\\qquad 0\\le A_\\sigma\\le CA_\\psi.\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\) there is \\(\\delta=\\delta(d,r,C,\\lambda)&gt;0\\) such that if \\(u\\ne0\\) is integral-valued divisorial on \\(Q\\) and\n\\[\n\\inf_{w|_Q=u}A_\\psi(w)&lt;\\delta,\n\\]\nthere is integral-valued divisorial \\(v\\) on \\(P\\) with\n\\[\nv|_Q\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}u,\\qquad A_\\psi(v)&lt;\\lambda.\n\\]\nFor relative dimension zero, regularity means \\(P=Q\\).</p><p>Mixing. Let \\(P\\) have a projective Fano type model over \\(\\mathbb C\\) of dimension at most \\(d\\). Let \\(\\sigma,\\psi,\\chi\\) have effective boundaries there, \\(\\sigma\\) have index at most \\(r\\), and\n\\[\nA_\\psi&gt;0,\\qquad 0\\le A_\\sigma\\le CA_\\psi.\n\\]\nIf, for some \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nA_\\chi(w)\\ge cA_\\psi(w)\n\\quad\\text{on }A_\\sigma(w)=0,\\ w\\ne0,\n\\]\nthen a rational \\(a\\in(0,1)\\), depending only on \\(d,r,C,c\\), satisfies\n\\[\n(1-a)A_\\psi(w)+aA_\\chi(w)&gt;0\n\\]\nfor every nonzero divisorial \\(w\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For Lifting, induct on \\(\\operatorname{trdeg}_Q P\\). At zero, \\(P=Q\\), so take \\(v=u\\) and \\(\\delta=\\lambda\\). Otherwise apply Proposition 6, obtaining \\(P/R\\) with geometrically \\(1/10\\)-lc Fano generic fibre. Propositions 5 and 1 give effective forms on \\(R\\) with\n\\[\n\\tfrac12\\bar A_\\sigma\\le A_{\\sigma_R}\\le\\bar A_\\sigma,\n\\qquad\n\\tfrac12\\bar A_\\psi\\le A_{\\psi_R}\\le\\bar A_\\psi,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma_R\\) has bounded index and\n\\[\n0\\le A_{\\sigma_R}\\le2CA_{\\psi_R},\\qquad A_{\\psi_R}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\delta_B\\) be the threshold from Proposition 5, part 2, for target \\(\\lambda\\). If \\(R=Q\\), apply it directly. Otherwise apply induction to \\(R/Q\\), with target \\(\\delta_B/2\\). The inequality\n\\[\n\\inf_{z|_Q=u}A_{\\psi_R}(z)\n\\le\\inf_{w|_Q=u}A_\\psi(w)\n\\]\nallows induction to produce integral-valued \\(z\\), restricting to a positive multiple of \\(u\\), with \\(A_{\\psi_R}(z)&lt;\\delta_B/2\\). Then \\(\\bar A_\\psi(z)&lt;\\delta_B\\), and bounded-fibre lifting gives \\(v\\). At most \\(d\\) tower steps occur; the reference index changes by a bounded function and \\(C\\) doubles at each step.</p><p>For Mixing, induct on \\(\\operatorname{trdeg}_{\\mathbb C}P\\). Proposition 6 and Proposition 5, part 3, give\n\\[\nA_\\chi\\ge cA_\\psi-DA_\\sigma\n\\]\non valuations trivial on \\(R\\) and those with \\(\\bar A_\\sigma(w|_R)=0\\). Choose rational \\(b\\in(0,1)\\) with \\(b(1+DC)\\le1/2\\), and put\n\\[\nA_{\\chi^+}=(1-b)A_\\psi+bA_\\chi.\n\\]\nOn those valuations,\n\\[\nA_{\\chi^+}\\ge\\tfrac12A_\\psi&gt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(R=\\mathbb C\\), this proves the result.</p><p>Otherwise \\(\\chi^+\\) is klt on the generic model over \\(R\\). Substitute its base form using Proposition 1 with anchor \\(\\psi\\) and \\(\\gamma=1/4\\):\n\\[\n\\bar A_{\\chi^+}-\\tfrac14\\bar A_\\psi\n\\le A_{\\chi_R}\\le\\bar A_{\\chi^+}.\n\\]\nOn \\(A_{\\sigma_R}=0\\),\n\\[\nA_{\\chi_R}\\ge\\tfrac14\\bar A_\\psi\\ge\\tfrac14A_{\\psi_R}.\n\\]\nThe forms have effective traces on the Fano type model \\(U\\). Induction gives \\(a_R\\in(0,1)\\) such that\n\\[\n(1-a_R)A_{\\psi_R}+a_RA_{\\chi_R}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor valuations with nonzero restriction this lifts by the upper bounds on substituted discrepancies; for trivial restriction positivity was already proved. Expanding gives the desired coefficient \\(a=a_Rb\\). Dimension decreases and all constants remain controlled.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 5.2, Integral jump", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After passage to a subsequence there is a uniform \\(C'\\) such that \\( w(J)\\le C'(A(w)+l_w) \\) for every such \\(J\\) and every divisorial \\(w\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive dimension.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider a sequence of smooth projective varieties \\(F\\), over algebraically closed fields isomorphic to \\(\\mathbb C\\), with projective birational morphisms \\(F\\to F_0\\), where \\(F_0\\) is normal.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\eta_F\\) be an lc rational volume form, of index one.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the pullback of a big semiample effective \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier divisor on \\(F_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The supports of \\(\\operatorname{div}(\\eta_F)\\) and \\(L\\) are SNC. Write \\(A=A_{\\eta_F}\\) and \\(l_w=w(L)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume:\n  \\[\n  A(w)=0\\Longrightarrow l_w&gt;0;\n  \\]\n  at every prime \\(P\\) on \\(F_0\\),\n  \\[\n  l_P=0\\Longrightarrow A(P)=1,\n  \\qquad\n  l_P&gt;0\\Longrightarrow A(P)\\le\\epsilon l_P,\n  \\quad\\epsilon\\to0;\n  \\]\n  for every rational \\(0&lt;b\\le1/10\\),\n  \\[\n  h^0(F,K_F+\\lceil bL\\rceil)=1;\n  \\]\n  and, uniformly for all effective \\(J\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}L\\),\n  \\[\n  w(J)\\le Cl_w\\quad\\text{on }A(w)=0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After passage to a subsequence there is a uniform \\(C'\\) such that\n\\[\nw(J)\\le C'(A(w)+l_w)\n\\]\nfor every such \\(J\\) and every divisorial \\(w\\). It suffices that the hypotheses hold sufficiently far along the sequence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(t\\) as in Lemma 8.2. Then \\(Y\\) is Fano type and \\(N=0\\). For effective \\(J\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}L\\), define\n\\[\nA_{\\chi_0}(w)=A(w)+tl_w-tw(J).\n\\]\nIts boundary on \\(Y\\) is effective. On \\(A=0\\), the clipping hypothesis and Lemma 8.1 give\n\\[\nA_{\\chi_0}\\ge-2(C-1)_+A_\\psi.\n\\]\nChoose uniform rational \\(\\theta\\in(0,1)\\) so that\n\\[\nA_\\chi=(1-\\theta)A_\\psi+\\theta A_{\\chi_0}\n\\ge\\tfrac12A_\\psi\n\\quad\\text{on }A_\\sigma=0.\n\\]\nProposition 7, Mixing, gives uniform rational \\(a\\in(0,1)\\). With \\(a_0=a\\theta\\),\n\\[\n0&lt;A(w)+tl_w-(1-a_0)w(H)-a_0tw(J).\n\\]\nSince \\(H\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nw(J)\\le\\frac{A(w)+tl_w}{a_0t}\n\\le\\frac{\\max\\{1,t\\}}{a_0t}(A(w)+l_w).\n\\]\nAll constants are uniform after the stated subsequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Section 9.1, Integral barrier", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are forms \\(\\sigma,\\psi\\) satisfying \\( A_\\sigma=A,\\qquad A_\\psi(w)=A(w)+tl_w-w(H), \\) and \\( A_\\psi(w)\\ge\\tfrac12(A(w)+tl_w)>0, \\qquad A_\\sigma\\le2A_\\psi. \\) The big-boundary good minimal model \\(Y\\) of …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 8, fix rational \\(t&gt;0\\), take \\(K_F=\\operatorname{div}(\\eta_F)\\), and put\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\lceil tL\\rceil-tL,\\qquad E=K_F+\\lceil tL\\rceil.\n\\]\nChoose \\(H=H_q/q\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}tL\\) general, with \\(q\\) sufficiently divisible.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are forms \\(\\sigma,\\psi\\) satisfying\n\\[\nA_\\sigma=A,\\qquad\nA_\\psi(w)=A(w)+tl_w-w(H),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nA_\\psi(w)\\ge\\tfrac12(A(w)+tl_w)&gt;0,\n\\qquad A_\\sigma\\le2A_\\psi.\n\\]\nThe big-boundary good minimal model \\(Y\\) of \\((F,\\Delta+H)\\) has semiample contraction \\(h:Y\\to S\\) and\n\\[\nE_Y=h^*N,\\qquad w(E)\\ge w(h^*N),\n\\]\nwhere \\(N\\ge0\\) is ample if \\(\\dim S&gt;0\\), and \\(N=0\\) if \\(S\\) is a point. Every integral-valued divisorial \\(w\\) satisfies\n\\[\nw(h^*N)&gt;0\\Longrightarrow A_\\sigma(w)+tl_w\\ge1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The boundary \\(\\Delta\\) has SNC coefficients in \\([0,1)\\). Since the form is lc of index one, the coefficients of \\(K_F\\) are integers at least \\(-1\\). A coefficient \\(-1\\) has positive \\(L\\)-coefficient, so \\(E\\) is effective integral.</p><p>Choose \\(H_q\\) smooth and transverse, with \\(q\\ge2\\), so that \\((F,\\Delta+2H)\\) is sub-lc. Then\n\\[\nw(H)\\le\\tfrac12A_{F,\\Delta}(w),\n\\qquad\nA_{F,\\Delta}(w)=A(w)+tl_w-w(E).\n\\]\nSince \\(E\\ge0\\), this gives the stated lower bound for \\(A_\\psi\\). The form exists because \\(tL-H\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}0\\).</p><p>The effective big-boundary klt pair has adjoint \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-linearly equivalent to \\(E\\ge0\\), so BCHM gives a good minimal model. Its effective semiample transform is the pullback of an effective ample \\(N\\), with exact divisor equality obtained by descending its defining section. MMP discrepancies give the pullback inequality.</p><p>If the barrier failed, the nonnegative integer \\(A_\\sigma(w)\\) would be zero. Let \\(D\\) be the reduced SNC union of the supports of \\(K_F,L\\). Then\n\\[\n0=A_\\sigma(w)=A_{F,D}(w)+\\sum_{P\\subset D}(1+k_P)w(P),\n\\]\nwhere \\(k_P=\\operatorname{coeff}_P(K_F)\\). All summands are nonnegative. Every seen component therefore has \\(k_P=-1\\) and \\(l_P&gt;0\\). Integral-valuedness and \\(tl_w&lt;1\\) imply\n\\[\n0&lt;tl_P&lt;1,\\qquad \\operatorname{coeff}_P(E)=-1+\\lceil tl_P\\rceil=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(w(E)=0\\), contradicting \\(w(h^*N)&gt;0\\). This unit barrier is where index one is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Sections 9.2–9.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Lemma 8.1, there is an integer \\(m\\), independent of \\(t\\), such that if \\(\\dim S>0\\), then on a suitable model \\(S'\\to S\\), the system \\(|\\lfloor mN'\\rfloor|\\) is birational, where \\(N'\\) is the pullback of \\(N\\).", "statement": "<p>In Lemma 8.1, there is an integer \\(m\\), independent of \\(t\\), such that if \\(\\dim S&gt;0\\), then on a suitable model \\(S'\\to S\\), the system \\(|\\lfloor mN'\\rfloor|\\) is birational, where \\(N'\\) is the pullback of \\(N\\).</p><p>Consequently, after fixing sufficiently small rational \\(t&gt;0\\) and passing far enough along the sequence, \\(S\\) is a point.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the generic fibre of \\(h\\), the effective boundaries for \\(\\sigma,\\psi\\) are \\(\\Delta_Y+tL_Y\\) and \\(\\Delta_Y+H_Y\\). The latter is klt log Calabi–Yau with big boundary, hence of Fano type by a Kodaira decomposition and small boundary perturbation.</p><p>Apply Proposition 7, Lifting, with index one, comparison constant \\(2\\), and target \\(1/4\\). The barrier implies that integral-valued valuations over a base valuation with \\(u(N)&gt;0\\) have \\(A_\\psi\\ge1/2\\). Hence uniformly in \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\bar A_\\psi(u)\\ge\\delta&gt;0\n\\quad\\text{for integral-valued }u\\text{ with }u(N)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The canonical bundle formula for \\((Y,\\Delta_Y+H_Y)\\) has generalized base discrepancy\n\\[\n\\beta(u)=\\bar A_\\psi(u)-u(N)&gt;0.\n\\]\nAttainment follows from the DVR threshold minimum. Rank one follows from the effective klt generic boundary and connected generic fibre. Replace the moduli part using Proposition 1 to obtain an effective klt boundary \\(B_S\\) with\n\\[\nK_S+B_S\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}N,\\qquad\n\\tfrac12\\beta\\le A_{S,B_S}\\le\\beta.\n\\]</p><p>Take a small \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorialization, and extract all primes with underlying discrepancy below\n\\[\n0&lt;\\epsilon'&lt;\\min\\{1,\\delta/4\\}\n\\]\nusing Lemma 6.1. The resulting \\(S'\\) is \\(\\epsilon'\\)-lc and\n\\[\nN'\\ge0,\\quad N'\\text{ nef and big},\\quad\nN'-K_{S'}\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}B_{S'}\\ge0.\n\\]\nEvery positive coefficient of \\(N'\\) is at least \\(\\delta/2\\). For an extracted prime,\n\\[\n\\beta(u)\\le2A_{S,B_S}(u)&lt;2\\epsilon',\n\\]\nso \\(u(N)\\ge\\delta-2\\epsilon'&gt;\\delta/2\\). For a prime already on the small model, choose a source prime of multiplicity \\(k\\). Since \\(E_Y\\) is the pushforward of integral \\(E\\), \\(ku(N)\\) is a positive integer, while\n\\[\n\\beta(u)\\le1/k\\le u(N).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\delta\\le u(N)+\\beta(u)\\le2u(N)\\).</p><p>Birkar’s effective birationality theorem applies to the \\(\\epsilon'\\)-lc variety polarized by \\(N'\\), using the decomposition \\(0+N'\\). It gives a uniform \\(m\\) with \\(|\\lfloor mN'\\rfloor|\\) birational.</p><p>Now fix \\(t\\) with \\(mt&lt;1/20\\), then pass far enough that\n\\[\nc_0=m(t+\\epsilon)&lt;1/10.\n\\]\nNonconstancy provides \\(g\\ne\\text{constant}\\) with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}(g)+mE\\ge0\n\\]\non \\(F\\). At a prime of \\(F_0\\), the coefficient of the pushforward of \\(E\\) is zero if \\(l_P=0\\), and otherwise at most\n\\[\nA(P)-1+\\lceil tl_P\\rceil\n\\le(t+\\epsilon)l_P.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}(g)+c_0L_0\\ge0.\n\\]\nPull back to \\(F\\). For \\(b=1/10\\), if \\(l_P&gt;0\\) and \\(n=\\operatorname{ord}_P(g)\\in\\mathbb Z\\), then\n\\[\nn\\ge-c_0l_P&gt;-bl_P\n\\Longrightarrow n\\ge1-\\lceil bl_P\\rceil.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(K_{F,P}\\ge-1\\), this makes \\(g\\) a section of \\(K_F+\\lceil bL\\rceil\\). If \\(l_P=0\\), then \\(n\\ge0\\) and \\(K_{F,P}\\ge0\\), by the lc-place hypothesis. The same holds for \\(1\\). These independent sections contradict \\(h^0=1\\). Therefore \\(S\\) is a point.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Section 4, including Proposition 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an invariant rational divisorial valuation \\( v=a^{-1}v_{\\mathrm{int}},\\qquad a=A_{\\theta_V}(v_{\\mathrm{int}})\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\qquad A_{\\theta_V}(v)=1, \\) whose associated graded algebra \\(R\\) is a finitely …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider a sequence of pointed normal Gorenstein klt germs \\((V,o)\\) over \\(\\mathbb C\\), of fixed dimension \\(n\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A finite cyclic group fixes \\(o\\), and \\(\\theta_V\\) is a semi-invariant canonical generator with zero divisor near \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Discrepancies are homogeneous. For a volume form,\n  \\[\n  A_\\eta(c\\operatorname{ord}_E)=c(1+\\operatorname{ord}_E\\eta).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Normalized valuation volume is \\(A(v)^n\\operatorname{vol}(v)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an invariant rational divisorial valuation\n\\[\nv=a^{-1}v_{\\mathrm{int}},\\qquad\na=A_{\\theta_V}(v_{\\mathrm{int}})\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0},\\qquad A_{\\theta_V}(v)=1,\n\\]\nwhose associated graded algebra \\(R\\) is a finitely generated Gorenstein klt cone algebra. Integral degree \\(I\\) means normalized degree \\(I/a\\).</p><p>For suitable centred regular eigenfunction generators \\(h_i\\), every regular \\(g\\ne0\\) and centred divisorial or real quasi-monomial \\(w\\) satisfy\n\\[\nw(g)\\le2nA_{\\theta_V}(w)v(g),\n\\qquad\nw(g)\\ge s(w)v(g),\n\\quad\ns(w)=\\min_i\\frac{w(h_i)}{v(h_i)}.\n\\]\nEvery nonzero positive-degree \\(h_I\\in R_I\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{lct}_o(\\operatorname{Spec}R;h_I)\n\\ge\\frac1{2n(I/a)};\n\\]\nin particular \\(I/a\\ge1/(2n)\\).</p><p>There is a bounded positive integer \\(N\\) and homogeneous \\(f_*\\in R_{aN}\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) with\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{Spec}R,N^{-1}\\operatorname{div}f_*)\\text{ lc}.\n\\]\nAfter normalizing a component of \\(u^{aN}=f_*\\), let \\(\\widehat E=\\operatorname{Proj}R'\\), \\(D=aH_u\\), and define \\(\\eta\\) by\n\\[\n\\theta=u^a\\eta\\wedge d\\log u.\n\\]\nThen \\(D\\) is ample effective \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier, \\(\\eta\\) is lc and is klt with zero divisor off \\(D\\), and, for \\(h=\\operatorname{vol}(v)\\),\n\\[\nh=a^nH^{n-1},\\qquad D^{n-1}\\le Nh.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 9.1 gives the equivariant rational Gorenstein cone degeneration. Lemma 9.2 proves Proposition 4.1 of the paper. Lemma 9.3 supplies the bounded complement and projective root-cover slice used below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Section 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The invariant normalized-volume minimizer admits a nearby rational monomial regrading with the same graded algebra and cyclic action on the whole local algebra.", "statement": "<p>The invariant normalized-volume minimizer admits a nearby rational monomial regrading with the same graded algebra and cyclic action on the whole local algebra. Its extended Rees family is finite type, normal, flat, and Gorenstein, with canonical residue generator of integral weight \\(a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Existence, quasi-monomiality, uniqueness, and finite generation of the minimizing valuation give an invariant \\(v_*\\), normalized by \\(A(v_*)=1\\), and a finitely generated klt cone \\(R\\).</p><p>On an SNC face with positive rationally independent weights, choose regular semi-invariant lifts \\(h_i\\) of homogeneous generators, enlarged by maximal-ideal and affine generators. Each has a unique least exponent \\(\\alpha_i\\), unchanged on one common positive neighbourhood of the weights: only finitely many exponents can compete on a compact positive neighbourhood. For every product, the least exponent remains \\(\\sum\\beta_i\\alpha_i\\), since every other exponent differs by a sum of positive generator gaps.</p><p>Successive initial subtraction writes a regular function to arbitrarily high order as sums of homogeneous polynomials in the \\(h_i\\). Rational independence makes all least exponents in one subtraction group equal; its coefficient is nonzero. Distinct groups have distinct exponents, so new scalar ties do not cancel. Coordinatewise comparison makes all errors tend to infinite order for every nearby weight. The same holds after group translation, since the original minimizer is invariant. Thus the whole associated graded algebra and its action are unchanged.</p><p>Choose nearby rational weights and rescale to primitive integral \\(v_{\\mathrm{int}}\\). Gorenstein klt gives \\(a=A_{\\theta_V}(v_{\\mathrm{int}})\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}\\). Choose the normalized weights between half and twice those of \\(v_*\\).</p><p>The valuation ideals are generated by monomials in the \\(h_i\\). Successive subtraction proves this modulo arbitrarily high valuation; Izumi’s inequality and Krull intersection remove the error. Away from \\(o\\) both ideals are the unit ideal. Hence the Rees family is generated by the original algebra, \\(t\\), and \\(t^{-v_{\\mathrm{int}}(h_i)}h_i\\). It is flat, and normality follows from normality away from the central fibre and the normal Cohen–Macaulay Cartier central fibre.</p><p>The central order is the Gauss extension of \\(v_{\\mathrm{int}}\\) with \\(w(t)=1\\). The monomial discrepancy calculation shows\n\\[\nt^{-a}\\theta_V\\wedge dt/t\n\\]\nhas a simple pole on the central fibre and no other divisor. Residue gives a zero-divisor canonical generator \\(\\theta\\) of integral weight \\(a\\). Positive graded units are constants, so generators from different rational regradings differ by scalars. Continuity of the canonical-character pairing gives normalized canonical weight one for the original minimizing Reeb valuation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Proposition 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The threshold and valuation comparisons stated in Proposition 9 hold.", "statement": "<p>The threshold and valuation comparisons stated in Proposition 9 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a torus eigenfunction \\(f\\), take an invariant threshold-computing divisor \\(w\\), normalized by \\(A(w)=1\\), and put \\(c=w(f)\\). For a nearby rational normalized Reeb grading \\(\\zeta\\), define\n\\[\nu_s(h_\\chi)=\\zeta(h_\\chi)+sw(h_\\chi).\n\\]\nA logarithmic Gauss extension and the automorphism\n\\[\nh_\\chi\\mapsto h_\\chi z^{j(\\chi)}\n\\]\ngive \\(A(u_s)=1+s\\): the canonical character contributes one. Counting modulo consecutive powers of \\(f\\), with \\(d_\\zeta=\\zeta(f)\\), gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(u_s)\n\\le\\operatorname{vol}(\\zeta)\\frac{d_\\zeta}{d_\\zeta+sc}.\n\\]\nIndeed the cumulative Hilbert growth of \\(R/(f)\\) has coefficient\n\\(d_\\zeta\\operatorname{vol}(\\zeta)/(n-1)!\\), and summing its shifted growth gives the estimate.</p><p>Normalized-volume minimization and \\(\\zeta\\to v_*\\) yield\n\\[\nd_*+sc\\le(1+s)^nd_*.\n\\]\nLet \\(s\\downarrow0\\), obtaining \\(c\\le nd_*\\). A homogeneous element for the rational regrading degenerates to an extreme component for the original torus, whose original weight is at most \\(2I/a\\). Threshold semicontinuity gives the stated bound.</p><p>For a regular \\(g\\), the regular Rees function \\(t^{-v_{\\mathrm{int}}(g)}g\\) restricts to its initial. Inversion of adjunction along the normal Gorenstein central fibre transfers the threshold to the pointed general germ, giving the upper valuation comparison. Approximation on a log-smooth model treats real quasi-monomial valuations. The monomial generators of valuation ideals give the lower comparison. Finally a nonunit has threshold at most one, giving \\(I/a\\ge1/(2n)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Sections 4.3 and equations (4.8)–(4.12)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The bounded complement function and root-cover slice in Proposition 9 exist and satisfy the stated discrepancy and degree identities.", "statement": "<p>The bounded complement function and root-cover slice in Proposition 9 exist and satisfy the stated discrepancy and degree identities.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(E=\\operatorname{Proj}R\\), \\(H=\\mathcal O_E(1)\\), and let \\(B\\) have coefficient \\(1-1/e_P\\) at generic isotropy order \\(e_P\\). Homogeneous principal slices show that \\((E,B)\\) is klt. For a homogeneous rational element \\(s\\) of weight one,\n\\[\n\\theta=s^a\\omega\\wedge d\\log s,\n\\qquad\nK_E+B\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}-aH.\n\\]\nThe grading is effective because \\(v_{\\mathrm{int}}\\) is primitive.</p><p>The bounded-complement theorem for standard coefficients gives an lc complement \\(B^+\\ge B\\) with bounded \\(N\\) and \\(N(K_E+B^+)\\sim0\\). A rational pluri-form with divisor \\(-NB^+\\), multiplied by the logarithmic radial form, gives an lc \\(\\psi\\). Then\n\\[\nf_*=\\theta^N/\\psi\n\\]\nhas degree \\(aN\\), has no poles on the punctured cone, and extends by normality and \\(n\\ge2\\). Its discrepancy identity proves the required lc condition.</p><p>Normalize a component of \\(u^{aN}=f_*\\), giving \\(u\\) degree one. The cone field is \\(k(\\widehat E)(u)\\). Define \\(\\eta\\) by\n\\[\n\\theta=u^a\\eta\\wedge d\\log u.\n\\]\nThe \\(N\\)-th power of the logarithmic product is the pullback of \\(\\psi\\), so \\(\\eta\\) is lc. Off \\(u=0\\) the cone cover is étale and a product with a torus, giving klt and zero Weil divisor there.</p><p>Hilbert counting gives \\(h=a^nH^{n-1}\\). Each degree class has the same leading growth, by multiplication with fixed homogeneous elements and bounded shifts for the individual cone. The cone cover has degree at most \\(aN\\), equal to its Proj degree because both fraction fields have weight-one radial parameters. Therefore\n\\[\nD^{n-1}=a^{n-1}H_u^{n-1}\\le Nh.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 5.1, Product of independent degree scales", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After passage to a subsequence, \\( h\\,d_1\\cdots d_n\\le C \\) for a uniform constant.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the sequence of cones, normalized valuations, and slices of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_j\\) be the first positive normalized degree at which homogeneous functions of degree at most that value have transcendence rank at least \\(j\\), for \\(1\\le j\\le n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h=\\operatorname{vol}(v)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After passage to a subsequence,\n\\[\nh\\,d_1\\cdots d_n\\le C\n\\]\nfor a uniform constant.</p><p>For the proof, split the degrees into tiers: ratios within a tier are bounded, and ratios between consecutive tiers tend to infinity. Choose algebraically independent homogeneous\n\\[\nf_1=f_*,f_2,\\ldots,f_n\n\\]\nwith degrees \\(e_1=N,e_2,\\ldots,e_n\\), each \\(e_j\\) comparable to \\(d_j\\), such that every complete tier prefix is a transcendence basis below the next tier.</p><p>For a prefix \\(1,\\ldots,m\\), resolve the pencils\n\\[\n\\phi_j=f_j/u^{ae_j},\\qquad 2\\le j\\le m.\n\\]\nLet \\(F\\) be a connected geometric generic component, \\(F_0\\) its normal graph component, \\(q=n-m\\), and\n\\[\n\\eta_F=\\eta/(d\\phi_2\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\phi_m).\n\\]\nWrite \\(D_F\\) for the pullback of the effective ample slice divisor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 9 gives\n\\[\n1/(2n)\\le d_1\\le N.\n\\]\nAfter subsequence extraction, split adjacent ratios into bounded ratios or ratios tending to infinity, giving the tiers and the stated independent functions.</p><p>Lemma 10.3 starts the induction:\n\\[\nh\\prod_{j\\le m}d_j\\le CD_F^{n-m},\n\\]\nbecause all first-tier degrees are bounded. Across a tier of scale \\(T\\) and length \\(k_1\\), Lemma 10.4 yields\n\\[\nT^{k_1}D_F^q\\le CD_{F^+}^{q-k_1}.\n\\]\nEvery new degree is comparable to \\(T\\), so this inserts exactly its factors. The terminal component is a point of degree one. There are finitely many tiers, hence finitely many subsequence choices, proving \\(h\\prod_jd_j\\le C\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every prime \\(P\\) of \\(F_0\\), put \\(c_P=P(D_F)\\).", "statement": "<p>For every prime \\(P\\) of \\(F_0\\), put \\(c_P=P(D_F)\\). Then\n\\[\nc_P&gt;0\\Longrightarrow\nA_{\\eta_F}(P)\\le\\left(\\sum_{j\\le m}e_j-1\\right)c_P,\n\\qquad\nc_P=0\\Longrightarrow A_{\\eta_F}(P)=1.\n\\]\nMoreover \\(\\eta_F\\) is lc and\n\\[\nA_{\\eta_F}(w)=0\\Longrightarrow w(D_F)&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Resolve all pencil graphs and form supports together, and apply generic smoothness to every stratum. Relative discrepancy formulas restrict to the geometric generic fibres. Off \\(D\\) the form is klt, proving the last implication.</p><p>Spread \\(P\\) to a horizontal divisor \\(P_0\\) of the normalized graph. Finite extensions labelling the component are unramified there, and division by the base differentials preserves its order. If \\(c_P=0\\), the graph is unchanged off \\(D\\), where the form has zero divisor, so the discrepancy is one.</p><p>For \\(c_P&gt;0\\), let \\(S\\) be its centre on \\(\\widehat E\\), of codimension \\(r\\). Among the pencil residues, \\(r-1\\) are independent over \\(k(S)\\): normalization is finite over the graph and the residue transcendence-degree difference is \\(r-1\\).</p><p>Extend \\(P_0\\) by the Gauss value \\(\\nu(u)=c_P/a\\). It is nonnegative on homogeneous cone functions. Its affine centre has transcendence degree \\(n-r\\), algebraically related to \\(k(S)(\\tau)\\), where \\(\\tau\\) is a Gauss-transcendental residue of a positive-degree unit. Choose \\(n-r\\) regular cone functions with independent centre residues, and supplement them by \\(f_1\\) and the \\(r-1\\) selected \\(f_j\\).</p><p>After powers and monomial changes, their logarithmic wedge has discrepancy zero: one function has positive order and the other \\(n-1\\) are units with independent residues. Their ordinary wedge has discrepancy at most \\(\\sum_{j\\le m}e_jc_P\\). Dividing it by the zero-divisor canonical generator is regular on the normal cone. Meanwhile\n\\[\nA_\\theta(\\nu)=A_{\\eta_F}(P)+c_P.\n\\]\nNonnegativity of the quotient’s value gives the estimate. The same calculation includes \\(m=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Suppose \\(q>0\\), the prefix is a complete tier prefix, and \\(T=d_{m+1}\\) starts the next tier.", "statement": "<p>Suppose \\(q&gt;0\\), the prefix is a complete tier prefix, and \\(T=d_{m+1}\\) starts the next tier. Uniformly sufficiently far along the jump,\n\\[\nh^0(F,K_F+\\lceil bTD_F\\rceil)=1\n\\qquad(0&lt;b\\le1/10,\\ b\\in\\mathbb Q).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The lc form \\(\\eta_F\\) supplies one section: its simple poles lie over \\(D_F\\). Suppose a second section has nonconstant ratio with it. Regard \\(F\\) as a component of a general complete intersection of the free pencils and extend by zero on the other components.</p><p>Successive adjunction lifts the section to\n\\[\nK_W+\\lceil bTD_W\\rceil+\\sum_{j=2}^mM_j,\n\\]\nwhere \\(M_j\\) is the pole member of the \\(j\\)-th free pencil. At each restriction, Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing applies to the SNC fractional boundary and the big nef divisor \\(bTD_Y\\) plus the remaining pencil divisors. Ceilings commute with the general cuts by the common resolution and generic smoothness.</p><p>Write the lift as \\(H'\\eta\\). Lemma 10.1 for \\(m=1\\), the ceiling excess, and \\(M_j\\le e_jD\\) bound its poles on \\(\\widehat E\\) by\n\\[\n\\left(bT+N+\\sum_{j=2}^me_j\\right)D.\n\\]\nSplit \\(H'\\) into deck-character parts of the cyclic root-field extension. Each has the same pole bound. For each part choose a cancelling exponent \\(I\\) with\n\\[\nbT+N+\\sum_{j=2}^me_j\n\\le I/a\n&lt;\nbT+N+\\sum_{j=2}^me_j+N+1/a.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(u^I\\) gives an invariant homogeneous regular cone function, by the pole bound and normality.</p><p>Far along the jump, \\(I/a&lt;T\\), uniformly for the stated \\(b\\). Every basis element of \\(R_I\\) is algebraic over \\(k(f_1,\\ldots,f_m)\\). Dividing by \\(u^I\\), and expanding a relation in the transcendental radial parameter \\(u\\), shows algebraicity over \\(k(\\phi_2,\\ldots,\\phi_m)\\). Thus each deck part is constant on \\(F\\), contradicting the nonconstant ratio.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Section 5.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the complete bounded first tier, of length \\(m\\), with \\(q=n-m\\), \\( h\\le CD_F^q,\\qquad D_F^q\\le Ch, \\) where the degree on a zero-dimensional geometric component is one.", "statement": "<p>For the complete bounded first tier, of length \\(m\\), with \\(q=n-m\\),\n\\[\nh\\le CD_F^q,\\qquad D_F^q\\le Ch,\n\\]\nwhere the degree on a zero-dimensional geometric component is one.</p><p>If \\(q&gt;0\\), every lc place \\(P\\) of \\(\\eta_F\\) and effective \\(J\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}D_F\\) satisfy\n\\[\nP(J)\\le CP(D_F).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Nef intersection with the pencil divisors, using \\(e_jD_W-M_j\\ge0\\), gives\n\\[\nD_F^q\\le D_W^q\\prod_{j=2}^mM_j\n\\le\\left(\\prod_{j=2}^me_j\\right)D_W^{n-1}\\le Ch.\n\\]</p><p>For the reverse inequality, test the full space \\(R_{\\le ap}\\). On \\(W\\times\\mathbb G_m\\), with coordinate \\(s\\), write\n\\[\nH_s=\\sum_{i\\le ap}s^ih_i^\\circ/u^i.\n\\]\nIt has pole bound \\(pD_W\\), and\n\\[\nw(H_s)\\le Cp\\bigl(A_{\\eta\\wedge d\\log s}(w)+w(D_W)\\bigr).\n\\]\nTo obtain this, add a Gauss variable \\(z\\) of weight\n\\(\\gamma&gt;w(D_W)/a\\), dilate \\(s\\mapsto sz\\), and test\n\\(\\sum z^{I-i}h_i^\\circ\\), where \\(I\\) is the largest degree. Its special fibre threshold is at least \\(1/(2np)\\) by Proposition 9. Inversion of adjunction gives\n\\[\nw(H_s)+I\\gamma\n\\le2np(A_{\\eta\\wedge d\\log s}(w)+a\\gamma).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\gamma\\downarrow w(D_W)/a\\).</p><p>Spread the selected component, and an extracted lc divisor when needed, after a finite generically étale base change. For the component test, use weight one on the \\(m\\) base parameters, including \\(s-s_0\\); discrepancy is \\(m\\) and divisor value zero. For the divisor test, add weight \\(1/c_P\\) on its equation, where \\(c_P=P(D_F)&gt;0\\); discrepancy remains \\(m\\), and divisor value becomes one.</p><p>Use the pushforward bundle of \\(\\mathcal O(pD_W)\\) for the component test. For the divisor test use the image sheaf of restriction to \\(jP\\), with \\(j=\\lceil Kpc_P\\rceil\\), whose generic rank is\n\\[\nh^0(F,pD_F)-h^0(F,pD_F-jP).\n\\]\nThe section-order bound injects the test space into jets of order \\(\\lceil Kp\\rceil\\): jet vanishing places a section in the sum of the base-jet ideal and the \\(P\\)-thickening ideal, both of weighted order at least \\(Kp\\). Jets in \\(m\\) parameters have dimension at most \\(C p^m\\) times the rank.</p><p>Since \\(\\dim R_{\\le ap}\\sim hp^n/n!\\), the first test gives \\(h\\le CD_F^q\\). The second gives\n\\[\nh\\le C\\bigl[D_F^q-\\operatorname{vol}(D_F-Kc_PP)\\bigr].\n\\]\nRounding is removed by continuity of volume on the fixed extraction.</p><p>If \\(t=P(J)&gt;Kc_P\\), put \\(\\lambda=Kc_P/t\\). Then\n\\[\nD_F-Kc_PP\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}(1-\\lambda)D_F+\\lambda J-Kc_PP,\n\\]\nwith the last part effective. Hence its volume is at least \\((1-\\lambda)^qD_F^q\\). The two volume comparisons force a uniform positive lower bound for \\(\\lambda\\), proving clipping.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "Section 5.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( L^q\\le C(L|_{F^+})^{q-k_1}, \\qquad (L|_{F^+})^{q-k_1}\\le CL^q. \\) If \\(\\dim F^+>0\\), clipping also holds on \\(F^+\\).", "statement": "<p>Suppose the clipping conclusion holds on a positive-dimensional prefix component \\(F\\), and the next tier has scale \\(T\\) and length \\(k_1\\). Let \\(F^+\\) be a compatible geometric generic component after the new pencils, and put \\(L=TD_F\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nL^q\\le C(L|_{F^+})^{q-k_1},\n\\qquad\n(L|_{F^+})^{q-k_1}\\le CL^q.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\dim F^+&gt;0\\), clipping also holds on \\(F^+\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 8 to \\((F,\\eta_F,L)\\). The lc-place condition follows from Lemma 10.1; the prime discrepancy constant is\n\\[\n\\epsilon=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\le m}e_j-1}{T}\\to0;\n\\]\nthe single-section condition is Lemma 10.2, and the prior clipping gives the final hypothesis. Therefore\n\\[\nw(J)\\le C'(A_{\\eta_F}(w)+w(L))\n\\]\nfor all effective \\(J\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}L\\).</p><p>Repeat the jet argument of Lemma 10.3 on \\(F\\), testing all sections of \\(pL\\), now with only the \\(k_1\\) new base parameters. The component test gives the first volume inequality. Nef intersection with the new pencils, each bounded by \\(CL\\), gives the second. For an lc divisor on \\(F^+\\), use the image of restriction to its thickening and the same normalized weighted test; the volume-deficit argument gives clipping. If \\(F^+\\) is a point, its rank and degree are one and no divisor test is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Section 6, especially Proposition 6.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The minimum of \\( \\Phi(w)=A_\\eta(w)/s(w) \\) among these extensions is attained on a finite rational polyhedral subdivision of the SNC cone complex of a fixed smooth proper model.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work on one germ from Proposition 9; put \\(L=\\mathbb C(V)\\), \\(\\eta=\\theta_V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h_i\\) be centred affine eigenfunction generators, \\(v_i=v(h_i)&gt;0\\), and\n  \\[\n  s(w)=\\min_iw(h_i)/v_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose centred regular eigenfunctions \\(f_1^0,\\ldots,f_n^0\\) with algebraically independent \\(v\\)-initials attaining the degree scales \\(d_j\\). Thus\n  \\[\n  d_j\\ge\\delta&gt;0,\\qquad d_1\\le C,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{vol}(v)\\prod_jd_j\\le C.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix centred regular eigenfunctions \\(f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_r)\\) and positive \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-linearly independent weights \\(b=(b_1,\\ldots,b_r)\\), admitting a centred quasi-monomial extension.</p><p class=\"li\">“Gauss on a tuple” means that its homogeneous initials are algebraically independent.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The minimum of\n\\[\n\\Phi(w)=A_\\eta(w)/s(w)\n\\]\namong these extensions is attained on a finite rational polyhedral subdivision of the SNC cone complex of a fixed smooth proper model. Finitely many invariant regular detectors permit a selected minimizing vertex, by maximizing their total value.</p><p>At that vertex,\n\\[\nw(h)=m_h\\cdot b,\\qquad\nA_\\eta(w)=e\\cdot b,\\qquad\ns(w)=g_s\\cdot b,\n\\]\nwith rational coefficient vectors having bounded denominators for this fixed vertex. Its rational rank is \\(r\\); for every active generator,\n\\[\nm_{h_i}=v_i g_s.\n\\]</p><p>For the fixed germ and tuple, selection has finitely many piecewise semialgebraic branches in \\(b\\); on a segment they have a finite interval partition into rational-smooth ratio branches. At dependent weights, selection is initially relaxed without a Gauss condition. A compact limit of bounded-ratio selected vertices from independent positive weights remains centred, quasi-monomial, and Gauss.</p>", "proof": "<p>Resolve the supports of \\(h_i,f_j,\\eta\\). Retraction preserves their values and \\(s\\), remains Gauss because \\(b\\) is independent, and decreases discrepancy strictly off the complex by Lemma 11.3.</p><p>Construct detectors from invariant regular numerators and denominators of polynomial coefficients for local boundary parameters over \\(L^G\\). Invariant rational functions admit invariant regular denominators by multiplying translates. Once detector values are fixed, Newton root values leave finitely many possibilities for each parameter; hence joint detector fibres on the finite complex are finite.</p><p>Subdivide so all relevant values and \\(s\\) are linear. On the cones with \\(w(h_i)\\ge0\\), klt positivity on rational rays gives positivity and properness of \\(A_\\eta\\). Also\n\\[\ns\\le\\min_j b_j/v(f_j).\n\\]\nA bounded ratio sublevel is compact; its limit cannot have \\(s=0\\), since that would force \\(A_\\eta=0\\) and the zero valuation, contradicting \\(w(f)=b\\ne0\\). Thus the minimum and secondary detector maximum are attained.</p><p>On a cut polyhedron, the minimum locus is the compact face\n\\[\nA_\\eta-\\Phi_{\\min}s=0.\n\\]\nMaximizing the detector sum permits a vertex. Its coordinates solve rational linear equations with right side linear in \\(b\\), giving the rational coefficient formulas, denominator bound, and rational rank. Independence of \\(b\\) gives the active-generator identity.</p><p>There are finitely many vertex formulas, feasibility conditions, and ratio/detector comparisons, all semialgebraic. This gives the stated branch partition. At a compact limit, continuity from Lemma 11.2 preserves, for every polynomial \\(P\\),\n\\[\nw(P(f))=\\min_{\\alpha:c_\\alpha\\ne0}\\alpha\\cdot b.\n\\]\nThus the single limit valuation is Gauss even at dependent weights and admits approximate continuation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, Finite windows", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Set \\(d_{n+1}=+\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>Set \\(d_{n+1}=+\\infty\\). There is a uniform \\(C_W\\) such that\n\\[\nH(T):=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathcal O_{V,o}/(v&gt;T)\n\\le C_W\\prod_{j\\le m}(T/d_j)\n\\quad(d_m\\le T&lt;d_{m+1}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Refine \\(v\\) by a full-rank monomial valuation on the independent degree-zero residues of the Gauss field \\(\\mathbb C(f_1^0,\\ldots,f_n^0)\\), then extend to the finite residue-field extension. The composite valuation \\(\\tau\\) has residue field \\(\\mathbb C\\) and lattice \\(\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb Q^n\\) containing \\(\\mathbb Z^n\\), using \\(\\tau(f_j^0)\\) as coordinates.</p><p>For regular \\(l_1,\\ldots,l_n\\) with independent \\(\\tau\\)-values generating \\(\\Lambda\\), choose regular representatives of every class of \\(\\Gamma/\\Lambda\\). Their products with the \\(l_i\\)-monomials have distinct values. Asymptotic counting gives\n\\[\n[\\Gamma:\\Lambda]\\le\\operatorname{vol}(v)\\prod_i v(l_i).\n\\]\nTaking \\(l_i=f_i^0\\) bounds \\([\\Gamma:\\mathbb Z^n]\\). Replacing \\(f_j^0\\) by \\(g\\), when its \\(j\\)-th coordinate is nonzero, gives\n\\[\n|\\tau(g)_j|\\le C\\,v(g)/d_j.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(v(g)\\le T&lt;d_{m+1}\\), its initial is algebraic over the graded field of the first \\(m\\) initials. In a homogeneous relation, two least refined terms with distinct powers tie, forcing \\(\\tau(g)\\) into the rational span of the first \\(m\\) coordinate vectors. Count the bounded lattice box:\n\\[\nC\\prod_{j\\le m}(1+T/d_j)\n\\le C'\\prod_{j\\le m}(T/d_j).\n\\]\nOne-dimensional leaves bound the finite jet quotient by this number.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(w\\) is Gauss on a full tuple \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\), it is quasi-monomial and \\( A_\\eta(w)= w\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{d\\log t_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\log t_n}\\right). \\) This is compatible with finite field extensions and …", "statement": "<p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(w\\) is Gauss on a full tuple \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\), it is quasi-monomial and\n   \\[\n   A_\\eta(w)=\n   w\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{d\\log t_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\log t_n}\\right).\n   \\]\n   This is compatible with finite field extensions and pulled-back forms.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A quasi-monomial valuation Gauss on a partial tuple admits nearby quasi-monomial Gauss extensions for nearby assigned weights, whose discrepancies and values on any fixed finite subset converge. A centre at \\(o\\) can be retained.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. On a finite SNC cone complex, evaluation of a rational function is continuous, homogeneous, and rational piecewise linear, including faces.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a full tuple, make a unimodular monomial change so that the nonzero weights are rationally independent and the remaining weights vanish. Resolve supports and maps. Independent zero-weight residues bound the centre codimension above, and the nonzero weights bound it below. The centre is the generic SNC stratum of that codimension, and independent parameter weights force the monomial valuation. The vertical exponent determinant and horizontal residue differentials show that the logarithmic wedge is a unit log volume, proving the formula, also upstairs.</p><p>For continuation, complete the partial tuple to a full homogeneous-independent tuple and vary its prescribed weights. The whole field is finite over the tuple field. Values of algebraic elements are Newton-polygon root values and depend continuously on coefficient values. To track several elements in one extension, choose an integer-power product separating all distinct tuples in their finite candidate value sets. A root with the required limiting product value gives an embedding, extended to the whole field. Separation forces all tracked values to converge. Include the form coefficient and the \\(h_i\\); then discrepancy converges and positivity preserves the centre.</p><p>For piecewise linearity, write numerator and denominator in a completed monomial chart. Noetherianity of monomial ideals reduces each minimum to finitely many exponents. Setting weights to zero gives the containing-stratum value; monomial cutoff ideals are primary in the remaining parameters and unchanged by localization and contraction. This proves continuity on faces.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\((Q,D)\\) be smooth proper SNC with \\(\\operatorname{Supp}\\operatorname{div}_Q\\eta\\subset D\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\((Q,D)\\) be smooth proper SNC with \\(\\operatorname{Supp}\\operatorname{div}_Q\\eta\\subset D\\). Retraction of a quasi-monomial \\(w\\) to the boundary cone complex decreases \\(A_\\eta\\) by\n\\[\nA_{Q,D}(w)\\ge0,\n\\]\nstrictly unless \\(w\\) already belongs to that complex.</p>", "proof": "<p>The discrepancy splits into a boundary-linear contribution, retained by retraction, and \\(A_{Q,D}(w)\\). Subdivide toroidally respecting the smallest rational subspace of the weight vector, then take a regular refinement. The reduced pair is crepant and the positive normal weights are independent.</p><p>If the centre is not generic in its open toric stratum, choose a smooth transverse divisor through it. Adding that divisor preserves SNC lc and subtracts a strictly positive valuation, so \\(A_{Q,D}(w)&gt;0\\). If the centre is generic, independent parameter weights force the monomial valuation. Refined normal parameters are toric monomials, and weight-zero ratios give only the torus-lattice relations, identifying this equality case with the original subdivided complex.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, Cartier selection", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After spreading, every sufficiently large reduction of characteristic \\(p\\) admits one product \\(H\\) of regular eigenfunctions with \\( \\sigma=m_H=p\\varphi g_s+O(1), \\) where the error is independent of \\(p\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one germ, independent weight assignment, and selected vertex \\(w\\) from Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\varphi=\\Phi(w)&gt;0\\), and use the vectors \\(m_h,e,g_s\\) there.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix finitely many regular eigenfunctions \\(t_\\nu\\) whose \\(w\\)-initials are algebraic over the graded tuple field of \\(f\\), and finitely many real cutoffs \\(T&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All characteristic-zero algebraic data are fixed before reduction. Required characteristics may depend on these data.</p><p class=\"li\">Write characters additively.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After spreading, every sufficiently large reduction of characteristic \\(p\\) admits one product \\(H\\) of regular eigenfunctions with\n\\[\n\\sigma=m_H=p\\varphi g_s+O(1),\n\\]\nwhere the error is independent of \\(p\\).</p><p>For this same \\(H\\), every vector \\(j_\\nu\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\) admits a regular nonzero eigenfunction \\(R\\) and integers \\(0\\le k_i&lt;p\\) with\n\\[\npm_R=\\sigma+\\sum_\\nu j_\\nu m_{t_\\nu}+k-(p-1)e,\n\\]\n\\[\np\\chi_R=\\chi_H+\\sum_\\nu j_\\nu\\chi_{t_\\nu}\n+\\chi_{f^k}-(p-1)\\chi_\\eta.\n\\]</p><p>For every prescribed cutoff \\(T\\), any collection of these reduced regular eigenfunctions with distinct \\((\\text{value},\\text{character})\\) pairs and weight at most \\(s(w_{\\mathrm{orig}})T\\) has size at most the characteristic-zero number \\(H(T)\\) of Lemma 11.1.</p>", "proof": "<p>Replace \\(u_i\\) in Lemma 12.4 by \\(h_i^{M/v_i}\\), and \\(a_\\lambda'\\) by their regular eigenfunction lifts. This gives a product \\(H\\) with\n\\[\nm_H=k_pMg_s+O(1)=p\\varphi g_s+O(1).\n\\]</p><p>The normalized initials of the tracked \\(t_\\nu\\) are nonzero constants: after adjoining the initial roots, their degree is zero and they are algebraic over the degree-zero field \\(\\mathbb C\\). Spread these constant identities, the residual form identities, the Kummer diagram, the group action and roots of unity, and an SNC chart realizing the valuation. Exclude primes dividing \\(N_0|G|\\). The finite tracked values, coefficient lattice, and centred generators persist.</p><p>For \\(Q_p=H\\prod t_\\nu^{j_\\nu}f^k\\), choose \\(0\\le k_i&lt;p\\) making\n\\[\nm_{Q_p}+e\\in p(N_0^{-1}\\mathbb Z^r).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nR=C(Q_p\\eta)/\\eta.\n\\]\nCartier preserves regular top forms on the smooth locus; normality extends \\(R\\) across codimension two. The tuple \\(x,y\\) is a field \\(p\\)-basis: value-lattice independence and the separating residual \\(p\\)-basis give \\(p^n\\) independent monomials. In its logarithmic frame, Cartier extracts the coefficient with all exponents zero modulo \\(p\\). The chosen divisibility and\n\\[\nC\\!\\left(U_p\\prod(t_\\nu')^{j_\\nu}\\rho\\right)\\ne0\n\\]\nshow that this coefficient occurs at the lowest value. Hence \\(R\\ne0\\) and the stated value formula holds. Cartier semilinearity and equivariance give the character formula. The same \\(H\\) works for all powers, since the \\(t_\\nu'\\) are nonzero constants.</p><p>For each cutoff, spread finite generators of \\(I_T=(v&gt;T)\\), a high maximal-ideal-power containment, and group-stability identities. The quotient retains length \\(H(T)\\). Tracking those generators gives\n\\[\nI_{T,p}\\subset(w&gt;s(w_{\\mathrm{orig}})T).\n\\]\nA dependence among distinct value-character pairs below the cutoff splits into characters; within one character its distinct lowest value cannot cancel. This proves the final counting assertion without spreading the entire valuation \\(v\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Section 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(N_0\\) clearing the coefficient value lattice, adjoin \\(x_j^{N_0}=f_j\\), and extend \\(w\\) to \\(\\widetilde w\\) on \\(\\widetilde L\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(N_0\\) clearing the coefficient value lattice, adjoin \\(x_j^{N_0}=f_j\\), and extend \\(w\\) to \\(\\widetilde w\\) on \\(\\widetilde L\\). Its coefficient lattice is \\(N_0^{-1}\\mathbb Z^r\\).</p><p>For \\(a\\in\\widetilde L^*\\), define\n\\[\na'=\\operatorname{res}_{\\widetilde w}(ax^{-N_0m_a}).\n\\]\nThen \\(K_0=\\kappa(\\widetilde w)\\) is finitely generated of transcendence degree \\(q=n-r\\), generated by normalized initials of regular eigenfunctions from \\(L\\).</p><p>With valuation units \\(y_1,\\ldots,y_q\\) whose residues form a transcendence basis, write\n\\[\n\\eta=a\\,d\\log x_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\log x_r\n\\wedge d\\log y_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\log y_q.\n\\]\nThen \\(m_a=e\\), and\n\\[\n\\rho=a'\\,d\\log\\bar y_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\log\\bar y_q\n\\]\nis a well-defined nonzero residual volume form, independent of the unit lifts and residual basis.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite abelian group acting on a valued field, let \\(D\\) be the decomposition subgroup. Distinct orbit valuations are inequivalent. Given a \\(D\\)-invariant homogeneous initial, weak approximation gives a lift with that initial at \\(w\\) and strictly higher value at every other orbit valuation. Average over \\(D\\), then sum over its cosets. This lifts the initial invariantly.</p><p>Homogeneous initials split into \\(D\\)-characters, and characters extend to the finite abelian group. For the Kummer extension they are realized by monomials in the \\(x_j\\); divide by such monomials and apply invariant lifting. Thus the upstairs graded field is generated by monomial initials and initials from \\(L\\), proving the coefficient lattice assertion. For \\(L/L^G\\), a normal basis supplies rational eigenfunctions; multiplying denominators by translates expresses them as quotients of regular eigenfunctions. Their normalized initials generate \\(K_0\\).</p><p>Finite extension preserves the Abhyankar equality. A smooth-stratum description identifies \\(K_0\\) with a stratum function field, proving finite generation and its dimension. Lemma 11.2 gives \\(m_a=e\\).</p><p>For independence of \\(\\rho\\), resolve two choices together. At the codimension-\\(r\\) stratum, the vertical log exponent matrix of \\(x\\) is invertible; the residue of the horizontal frame determinant is the determinant changing the restricted differentials. The common normalization by \\(x^{-N_0e}\\) therefore gives the same residual form. For \\(q=0\\), \\(\\rho\\) is a nonzero scalar.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( A_\\rho(z)+\\varphi l(z)\\ge0, \\) strictly when \\(z\\ge0\\) on \\(A_0\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose a finitely generated \\(\\mathbb C\\)-algebra \\(A_0\\subset K_0\\), with fraction field \\(K_0\\), generated by normalized initials of regular eigenfunctions and containing those of every \\(h_i\\) and detector. Let \\(J\\) be the active generators and put\n\\[\nl(z)=-\\min_{i\\in J}z(h_i')/v_i\n\\]\nfor divisorial \\(z\\) of \\(K_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nA_\\rho(z)+\\varphi l(z)\\ge0,\n\\]\nstrictly when \\(z\\ge0\\) on \\(A_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the lexicographic composite \\((\\widetilde w,z)\\), adapting the residual tuple so that one residual variable is a parameter for \\(z\\) and the others have independent residues. Project its two weight rows to the first plus \\(t&gt;0\\) times the second.</p><p>Track simultaneously the form coefficient, all \\(h_i\\), all detectors, and the residual parameter. Their algebraic values have finitely many lexicographic Newton candidates. Choose an integer-power product separating the full Cartesian product of candidate tuples. For small \\(t\\), all comparisons defining its Newton polygon persist, so a root has the projected product value. Extend its embedding to \\(\\widetilde L\\); separation forces all prescribed projected values. This gives quasi-monomial extensions \\(w_t\\) with\n\\[\nA_\\eta(w_t)=A_\\eta(w)+tA_\\rho(z),\n\\qquad\ns(w_t)=s(w)-tl(z).\n\\]\nThey remain centred and retain the tuple weights. Minimality gives the nonnegative inequality.</p><p>If equality held and \\(z\\ge0\\) on \\(A_0\\), then every \\(w_t\\) would minimize the ratio. Strict retraction places it on the fixed complex. Detector values are nondecreasing; maximality of their sum makes them all constant. The joint detector fibre is finite, and each valuation has finitely many extensions to \\(\\widetilde L\\). This contradicts the varying residual-parameter value \\(t\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "Section 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose \\(M>0\\) with \\(M/v_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) for active \\(i\\), and put \\(u_i=(h_i')^{M/v_i}\\).", "statement": "<p>Choose \\(M&gt;0\\) with \\(M/v_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}\\) for active \\(i\\), and put \\(u_i=(h_i')^{M/v_i}\\). There are a smooth projective residual model \\(Z\\), an SNC divisor \\(B_Z\\), a Cartier divisor \\(D_0\\) generated by the \\(u_i\\), and an ample integral divisor \\(P\\) supported on \\(B_Z\\), such that\n\\[\nz(D_0)=Ml(z),\n\\]\n\\[\nP_T\\ge\\max\\{0,(D_0)_T\\}\\quad\\text{over infinity},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\beta/\\rho\\in A_0\n\\quad\n\\bigl(\\beta\\in H^0(Z,\\Omega_Z^q(\\log B_Z)(P))\\bigr),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH^a(Z,\\Omega_Z^i(\\log B_Z)(P+jD_0))=0\n\\]\nfor \\(a&gt;0\\), \\(0\\le i\\le q\\), and \\(-|J|\\le j\\le0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Resolve the rational map given by the \\(u_i\\); its generated divisor has order \\(-\\min_i z(u_i)=Ml(z)\\).</p><p>Normalize a projective closure of \\(\\operatorname{Spec}A_0\\), with ample Cartier infinity. On a smooth projective model resolving the \\(u_i\\), choose effective ample \\(P_0\\), a conductor \\(c_0\\in A_0\\), and \\(g\\in A_0\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) vanishing at all finite height-one primes below the supports of \\(D_0,P_0,\\operatorname{div}\\rho,\\operatorname{div}c_0\\). Resolve these supports and infinity by \\(\\mu:Z\\to Z_0\\). Choose effective exceptional \\(F\\) with \\(-F\\) relatively ample.</p><p>For \\(m\\) large, then \\(c\\gg m\\), then \\(h\\gg c\\), take a sufficiently large positive multiple of\n\\[\nm\\mu^*P_0-F-c\\operatorname{div}(g)+hH_\\infty.\n\\]\nIt is ample; the infinity coefficients dominate the required ones. At finite height-one primes it has arbitrarily negative coefficients where needed. Thus \\(\\beta/(\\rho c_0)\\) is regular in codimension one on the affine normalization. Normality and the conductor imply \\(\\beta/\\rho\\in A_0\\). Serre vanishing after another multiple gives the finite list of vanishings. For \\(q=0\\), take a point.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.4", "orig": "Section 7.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Spread the fixed data of Lemma 12.3 to sufficiently large characteristic \\(p\\), with a length-two Witt lift of the smooth SNC pair.", "statement": "<p>Spread the fixed data of Lemma 12.3 to sufficiently large characteristic \\(p\\), with a length-two Witt lift of the smooth SNC pair. Put\n\\[\nk_p=\\lfloor p\\varphi/M\\rfloor,\\qquad\nH_p=k_pD_0+P,\\qquad A_p=\\lfloor H_p/p\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\rho\\) is a nonzero section of\n\\[\n\\Omega_Z^q(\\log B_Z)(A_p),\n\\]\nand there is a monomial\n\\[\nU_p=u^\\alpha\\prod_\\lambda(a_\\lambda')^{I_\\lambda},\n\\qquad |\\alpha|=k_p,\n\\]\nwhere \\(I\\) belongs to a fixed finite set, such that\n\\[\nC(U_p\\rho)\\ne0\n\\]\nfor field Cartier \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The generating Koszul complex of the \\(u_i\\), together with the finite vanishings of Lemma 12.3, gives by induction, for \\(E=\\Omega_Z^i(\\log B_Z)(P)\\),\n\\[\nH^a(Z,E(dD_0))=0\\quad(a&gt;0,d\\ge0),\n\\qquad\nH^0(Z,E(dD_0))=\\sum_{|\\alpha|=d}u^\\alpha H^0(Z,E).\n\\]</p><p>At a boundary prime, membership of \\(\\rho\\) is equivalent, by integrality of \\(A_\\rho\\), to\n\\[\nA_\\rho(T)+(H_p)_T/p\\ge0.\n\\]\nIts limit is \\(A_\\rho(T)+(\\varphi/M)(D_0)_T\\). It is strictly positive over the finite part by Lemma 12.2; at infinity it is nonnegative and the rounding error is nonnegative by the choice of \\(P\\).</p><p>There is an inclusion of meromorphic log de Rham complexes\n\\[\n\\Omega_Z^\\bullet(\\log B_Z)(pA_p)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Omega_Z^\\bullet(\\log B_Z)(H_p).\n\\]\nAfter Frobenius pushforward it is a quasi-isomorphism. Locally, if an axis coefficient is \\(pa+r\\), \\(0\\le r&lt;p\\), the additional exponents have nonzero residue modulo \\(p\\); contraction by a log Euler field divided by that residue kills those summands. This also covers negative coefficients.</p><p>For \\(p&gt;q\\), logarithmic Deligne–Illusie splitting for the length-two lift gives a surjection from degree-\\(q\\) hypercohomology to\n\\[\nH^0(Z,\\Omega_Z^q(\\log B_Z)(A_p))\n\\]\nby Cartier. The larger complex has no higher sheaf cohomology by the Koszul consequence, so a global top section represents a class mapping to \\(\\rho\\), and has nonzero field Cartier image.</p><p>Expand this section by the displayed generation formula and the fixed polynomial expressions for top-section ratios in the \\(a_\\lambda'\\). Additivity and semilinearity of Cartier imply that one monomial term has nonzero image. Its auxiliary multi-index lies in the fixed finite support.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Theorem 3.1, Local phase assertion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After passage to a subsequence, there are centred divisorial valuations \\(w_i'\\), a uniform \\(C>0\\), and a positive integer \\(\\ell\\), independent of \\(i\\), such that \\( A_{\\theta_i}(w_i')\\le C \\) and, for every nonzero …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((V_i,o_i)\\) be pointed algebraic Gorenstein klt germs over \\(\\mathbb C\\), of fixed dimension \\(n\\ge2\\), with closed \\(o_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A finite cyclic group \\(G_i\\) acts on an affine representative, fixes \\(o_i\\), and has a semi-invariant zero-divisor canonical generator \\(\\theta_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose arbitrary \\(\\gamma_i\\in G_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a character \\(\\chi\\), its angle is in \\(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\), with value \\(\\exp(2\\pi i\\,\\operatorname{angle}\\chi)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After passage to a subsequence, there are centred divisorial valuations \\(w_i'\\), a uniform \\(C&gt;0\\), and a positive integer \\(\\ell\\), independent of \\(i\\), such that\n\\[\nA_{\\theta_i}(w_i')\\le C\n\\]\nand, for every nonzero rational semi-invariant \\(h\\) of character \\(\\chi\\),\n\\[\nw_i'(h)\\equiv\\ell\\,\\operatorname{angle}\\chi(\\gamma_i)\\pmod{\\mathbb Z}.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(w_i'\\) restricts to an integral-valued valuation of \\(\\mathbb C(V_i)^{G_i}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with the original full first-tier prefix at \\(v\\), ratio one, and continue slightly to independent weights. At every later tier, continue the previous exact target slightly to independent weights. While the tuple is incomplete, use Lemma 13.2 and continue within the prescribed tolerance. At each tier endpoint use Lemma 13.4. The prospective finite recursion makes all bounds uniform without circular dependence.</p><p>At the final tier obtain \\(n\\) centred regular eigenfunctions, positive rational targets, and a centred quasi-monomial Gauss valuation \\(w\\) satisfying\n\\[\nSb_i^*\\equiv\\vartheta_{f_i}\\pmod{\\mathbb Z},\\qquad\ns(w)\\ge c,\\quad A_\\eta(w)\\le C,\\quad b_i^*\\le B_*d_i.\n\\]\nA full Gauss tuple with positive rational weights is divisorial-scaled, by rank one and residue transcendence degree \\(n-1\\).</p><p>Choose uniform \\(D_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\) with\n\\[\n(D_{\\mathrm{fin}}+1)(c/(2nB_*))^n&gt;C_W.\n\\]\nFor a regular eigenfunction \\(h\\), choose \\(T\\ge d_n\\) large and consider\n\\[\nh^jf^\\alpha,\\qquad\n0\\le j\\le D_{\\mathrm{fin}},\\quad\n0\\le\\alpha_i\\le sT/(2nb_i^*).\n\\]\nThere are more indices than \\(H(T)\\). Increase \\(T\\) so that\n\\(D_{\\mathrm{fin}}w(h)\\le sT/2\\); all values are at most \\(sT\\). A dependence modulo \\((v&gt;T)\\) can be chosen in one character. Its lowest terms cancel, since the sum either vanishes or has value greater than \\(sT\\).</p><p>Two cancelling terms must have distinct powers \\(j,j'\\), since otherwise the Gauss initials of \\(f\\) would satisfy a polynomial relation. Equal values and characters give\n\\[\n(j-j')(Sw(h)-\\vartheta_h)=0\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,\n\\qquad 1\\le|j-j'|\\le D_{\\mathrm{fin}}.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\n\\ell=\\operatorname{lcm}(1,\\ldots,D_{\\mathrm{fin}}),\n\\qquad w'=\\ell Sw.\n\\]\nThis proves the congruence for every regular eigenfunction with the same \\(\\ell\\). For a rational semi-invariant, multiply a denominator by all its group translates to obtain an invariant regular denominator and subtract the two regular congruences. The discrepancy bound is \\(A_\\eta(w')\\le\\ell SC\\), uniformly. Restriction to invariant functions is integral-valued, proving the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly, \\( \\#\\{(m_t,\\chi_t)\\bmod\\Lambda_f\\}\\le C_3, \\qquad |m_{t,i}|\\le C_4w(t)/d_i. \\)", "statement": "<p>Use a selected vertex of Proposition 11. Let \\(m\\) end a tier, \\(m_-\\) end the preceding tier, \\(d=d_m\\), and suppose\n\\[\n\\max\\{1,m_-\\}\\le r\\le m,\\quad\nf_1=f_1^0,\\quad\n\\tfrac12\\le b_1/d_1\\le2,\\quad\nb_j\\le B_*d_j,\n\\]\nwith an available Gauss extension of ratio at most \\(M_*\\).</p><p>For regular eigenfunctions \\(t\\) with algebraic initial over the graded tuple field, put\n\\[\n\\Lambda_f=\\langle(e_i,\\chi_{f_i}):1\\le i\\le r\\rangle_{\\mathbb Z}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly,\n\\[\n\\#\\{(m_t,\\chi_t)\\bmod\\Lambda_f\\}\\le C_3,\n\\qquad\n|m_{t,i}|\\le C_4w(t)/d_i.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The anchor and valuation comparisons give\n\\[\nc\\le s(w)\\le2,\\qquad A_\\eta(w)\\le2M_*,\n\\qquad c=(4nM_*)^{-1}.\n\\]\nLet \\(D_j=\\prod_{i\\le j}(d/d_i)\\). For each fixed cutoff multiplier \\(K\\), omit a finite prefix so that \\(Kd\\) is below the next tier. Lemma 11.1 and tier comparability give\n\\[\nH(Kd)\\le C_WK^mD_m,\\qquad D_m\\le C_2D_r.\n\\]</p><p>For powers satisfying \\(\\sum_\\nu(j_\\nu/p)w(t_\\nu)\\le d\\), Proposition 12 gives outputs with\n\\[\nw(R)\\le(2+rB_*)d\n\\]\nfor large \\(p\\), since \\((\\varphi g_s-e)\\cdot b=0\\). Each box\n\\[\nRf^\\alpha,\\qquad 0\\le\\alpha_i\\le d/d_i,\n\\]\nhas at least \\(D_r\\) distinct exponent-character pairs and lies below \\(s(w)Kd\\) for uniform \\(K\\). The cutoff count permits only uniformly many pairwise disjoint boxes.</p><p>For distinct input classes, use power one on each test with the same \\(H\\). Equal output classes would, after subtracting the value and character equations, force equal input classes. No division in the character group is used. This proves the class bound.</p><p>For a fixed \\(t\\), vary \\(j/p\\) through \\([0,d/w(t)]\\). The \\(i\\)-th output coordinate is a common translate plus \\((j/p)m_{t,i}\\), with an error in \\([0,1)\\). An excessive \\(|m_{t,i}|d/w(t)\\) would give more than \\(C_3\\) disjoint boxes. Let \\(p\\) grow after fixing all tests and cutoffs. This gives the coordinate bound. If \\(w(t)=0\\), independence of \\(b\\) gives \\(m_t=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Lemma 13.1, if \\(r<m\\), there is a centred regular eigenfunction \\(t\\) with \\( w(t)\\le C_5d \\) whose initial is transcendental over the graded tuple field.", "statement": "<p>Under Lemma 13.1, if \\(r&lt;m\\), there is a centred regular eigenfunction \\(t\\) with\n\\[\nw(t)\\le C_5d\n\\]\nwhose initial is transcendental over the graded tuple field. Adjoining it preserves the Gauss property. The enlarged tuple can be continued to arbitrarily close independent weights with ratio increased by at most one.</p>", "proof": "<p>If all eigenfunction initials of weight at most \\(L_*d\\) were algebraic, each fixed value-character pair would span at most one initial dimension, by constant normalized residues. Lemma 13.1 bounds the number of pairs by\n\\[\nC(1+L_*)^rD_r.\n\\]</p><p>Now use the original reference tuple. The span of\n\\[\n(f_1^0)^{\\alpha_1}\\cdots(f_m^0)^{\\alpha_m},\n\\qquad 0\\le\\alpha_j\\le Dd/d_j,\n\\]\nhas dimension at least \\(D^mD_m\\). Every nonzero combination has \\(v\\)-value at most \\(mDd\\), hence \\(w\\)-value at most \\(C'Dd\\). Decompose into character subspaces and use an adapted basis for each finite-dimensional valuation filtration. If all initials were algebraic, their total dimension would be at most \\(C(1+C'D)^rD_r\\), impossible for a sufficiently large fixed \\(D\\) because \\(r&lt;m\\) and \\(D_m\\ge D_r\\).</p><p>The resulting \\(t\\) is a nonunit, and its transcendental initial enlarges the Gauss tuple. The next degree is in the same tier. Lemma 11.2 gives arbitrarily close independent continuation, retaining the centre and with arbitrarily small ratio increase.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Lemma 13.1, if \\(r=m\\), then \\( \\left|\\frac{(e-\\varphi g_s)_i}{s}\\right| \\le C_6d/d_i. \\) Consequently, on selected ratio branches at independent parameters …", "statement": "<p>Under Lemma 13.1, if \\(r=m\\), then\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{(e-\\varphi g_s)_i}{s}\\right|\n\\le C_6d/d_i.\n\\]\nConsequently, on selected ratio branches at independent parameters,\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{d\\Phi}{dt}\\right|\n\\le C_6\\sum_{i\\le m}(d/d_i)|\\dot b_i|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 12 with no tests. Its output satisfies\n\\[\nm_R=\\varphi g_s-e+k/p+o(1).\n\\]\nFor fixed large \\(D\\), consider\n\\[\nR^jf^\\alpha,\\qquad 0\\le j\\le D,\\quad\n0\\le\\alpha_i\\le Dd/d_i.\n\\]\nThey lie below a uniform cutoff \\(sKDd\\). If some \\(|m_{R,i}|&gt;Dd/d_i\\), these \\(D+1\\) boxes are disjoint in that coordinate and give at least \\(D^{m+1}D_m\\) distinct values. The cutoff bound is at most \\(C_WK^mD^mD_m\\). Fix \\(D&gt;C_WK^m\\), then let \\(p\\to\\infty\\). This bounds \\(|(\\varphi g_s-e)_i|\\); divide by the uniform lower bound for \\(s\\).</p><p>On a branch,\n\\[\n\\Phi=(e\\cdot b)/(g_s\\cdot b),\n\\]\nwhose derivative is \\(((e-\\varphi g_s)/s)\\cdot\\dot b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.4 and Section 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A sufficiently large common \\(S\\) ensures positivity, the anchor condition, and a weighted segment length \\( \\sum_{i\\le m}(d/d_i)|b_i^*-b_i|\\le C_7/S. \\) If \\(C_6C_7/S<1\\), an available ratio bound \\(M\\) at the start …", "statement": "<p>Fix all prospective ratio and weight budgets by a finite recursion before choosing a rounding integer \\(S\\). For a completed tier prefix, choose targets satisfying\n\\[\nSb_i^*\\equiv\\operatorname{angle}\\chi_{f_i}(\\gamma)\\pmod{\\mathbb Z},\n\\]\nretaining old exact targets and rounding new coordinates to nearest targets.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A sufficiently large common \\(S\\) ensures positivity, the anchor condition, and a weighted segment length\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\le m}(d/d_i)|b_i^*-b_i|\\le C_7/S.\n\\]\nIf \\(C_6C_7/S&lt;1\\), an available ratio bound \\(M\\) at the start gives a centred quasi-monomial Gauss extension at the exact target with ratio at most \\(M+1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>There are at most \\(n\\) enlargements and \\(n\\) tier rounds. Reserve one ratio unit for each possible continuation and round, and a stopping margin; choose \\(M_*\\) above the initial ratio one plus all reserves. Start a weight budget, for example \\(B_0=2\\). Recursively enlarge it using the already proved \\(C_5(B_j,M_*)\\), tier comparability, and fixed movement allowances. Take maxima of the finitely many derivative constants and cutoff multipliers. Only then choose \\(S\\), and finally discard a finite prefix to put all fixed cutoffs below the next tiers.</p><p>Use continuation tolerance\n\\[\n\\epsilon=d_1/(Sd_n)\n\\]\nper coordinate, or smaller. Old coordinates are within \\((n+1)\\epsilon\\) of their fixed targets; new rounding distances are at most \\(1/(2S)\\), and their \\(d/d_i\\) ratios are bounded. This gives the weighted-length estimate. The lower bounds for \\(s,d_1\\) keep every new coordinate and segment positive; the anchor remains in \\([d_1/2,2d_1]\\).</p><p>On a nonconstant segment starting at independent weights, independent parameters are dense. Use the finite semialgebraic branch partition of Proposition 11. Integrate Lemma 13.3 on feasible branches while the reserved bound holds. At a branch endpoint, compactness gives a Gauss limit by Proposition 11; continuation from that limit prevents loss of feasibility or an upward jump. The integrated bound is\n\\[\n\\Phi\\le M+tC_6C_7/S.\n\\]\nA first excess of \\(M+1\\) is impossible. The compact limit at the final endpoint supplies the asserted actual Gauss extension, despite possibly dependent target weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 3.2, Termination of smooth canonical programs", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The program terminates with nef canonical divisor.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be a smooth projective complex variety with pseudo-effective \\(K_X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose effective ample rational \\(H\\) such that \\((X,H)\\) is klt and \\(K_X+H\\) is ample.</p><p class=\"li\">Run a \\(K_X\\)-MMP with scaling of \\(H\\), with models \\(X_i\\), transforms \\(H_i\\), and scaling numbers \\(\\lambda_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The program terminates with nef canonical divisor. Consequently, on a smooth projective birational model \\(p:U\\to X\\),\n\\[\np^*K_X=P+N,\n\\qquad\nP\\text{ nef rational Cartier},\\quad\nN\\ge0\\text{ rational Cartier}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Dimensions zero and one are immediate. Suppose an infinite program in dimension \\(n\\ge2\\). Lemmas 14.1–14.4 apply.</p><p>Fix the common index \\(R\\). For a late flip choose very ample \\(J\\) on \\(Z_i\\) so positive that\n\\[\nD=-RK_{X_i}+f_i^*J,\\qquad D-K_{X_i}\n\\]\nare ample. Effective basepoint-freeness gives a uniform \\(N=N(n)\\) such that \\(L=ND\\) is globally generated and ample. Choose the stage late enough for Lemma 14.4 at the finite list\n\\[\nm=R+jNR,\\qquad1\\le j\\le n.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(a\\), projection formula, Leray, and Serre vanishing give\n\\[\nH^j(X_i,\\mathcal O_{X_i}(RK_{X_i}+af_i^*J-jL))=0\n\\quad(j&gt;0).\n\\]\nRegularity with respect to ample globally generated \\(L\\) makes\n\\(\\mathcal O_{X_i}(RK_{X_i}+af_i^*J)\\) globally generated. For the usual very ample formulation, push along the finite morphism defined by \\(L\\), apply regularity on projective space, and pull back. Its degree on an \\(f_i\\)-contracted curve is \\(RK_{X_i}\\cdot C&lt;0\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Pseudo-effectivity persists by pushing the big systems \\(K_X+tH\\) and taking limits. It rules out a negative fibre endpoint, so the finite endpoint \\(Y\\) has nef \\(K_Y\\). On a common smooth resolution,\n\\[\np^*K_X=q^*K_Y+N,\\qquad N\\ge0.\n\\]\nSet \\(P=q^*K_Y\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Proposition 3.2, equations (3.2)–(3.8) and “A uniform multiplier-ideal estimate.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\lambda_i\\to0\\).", "statement": "<p>Suppose the program in Proposition 14 is infinite, and put\n\\[\nA_i(u)=A_{X_i}(u),\\quad\nE_i(u)=A_i(u)-A_X(u),\\quad\n\\Delta_i(u)=A_{i+1}(u)-A_i(u)\\ge0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(\\lambda_i\\to0\\). There are fixed ideals \\(\\mathcal J_s\\subset\\mathcal O_X\\), for \\(s\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}\\), and indices \\(i(s)\\), independent of \\(u\\), such that for \\(i\\ge i(s)\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{u(\\mathcal J_s)}s\n\\le E_i(u)\\le\\sigma_u\n\\le\\frac{u(\\mathcal J_s)+A_X(u)}s,\n\\qquad\n0\\le\\Delta_i(u)\\le A_X(u)/s.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\sigma_u=\\lim_{t\\downarrow0}\\sigma_u(t),\\qquad\n\\sigma_u(t)=\\inf_m m^{-1}u(\\mathfrak b(|m(K_X+tH)|)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At step \\(j\\), pullbacks of \\(K+\\lambda_jH\\) agree by negativity, while the canonical pullback difference is effective. Summing gives, for \\(0&lt;t\\le\\lambda_i\\), effective exceptional difference\n\\[\nF_i(t;u)=\\sum_{j&lt;i}(1-t/\\lambda_j)\\Delta_j(u).\n\\]\nThus \\((X_i,tH_i)\\) is klt, its adjoint is big, and at \\(t=\\lambda_i\\) it is nef and semiample.</p><p>Only finitely many steps are divisorial. If \\(\\lambda_i\\) had positive limit, choose a positive compact interval below it on a fixed late model. Replace a small fixed portion of the big \\(H_j\\) by general ample divisors spanning \\(N^1\\) plus an effective remainder. The resulting klt big-adjoint boundaries lie in a rational polytope with common ample part. BCHM gives finitely many marked ample models. Every late small model is one after a small spanning perturbation of its nef scaling adjoint. Numerical spaces and sections are identified across small maps by negativity and codimension-one comparison. No marked model recurs, since a genuine flip strictly improves some discrepancy. This contradiction gives \\(\\lambda_i\\to0\\).</p><p>Semiampleness at \\(\\lambda_i\\) and the fixed-part identity give\n\\[\n\\sigma_u(\\lambda_i)\\le E_i(u)\\le\\sigma_u.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\mathcal J_s(t)=\\mathcal J(X,s\\|K_X+tH\\|)\\), choose a Cartier \\(P_s\\) so positive that\n\\[\nP_s-jH_0-K_X-s(K_X+tH)\n\\]\nis ample for \\(1\\le j\\le n\\), \\(0\\le t\\le1\\). Asymptotic Nadel vanishing and regularity make \\(\\mathcal J_s(t)(P_s)\\) globally generated. Their nested section spaces lie in a fixed finite-dimensional space, so stabilize as \\(t\\downarrow0\\); generation stabilizes the ideals to \\(\\mathcal J_s\\).</p><p>The multiplier-ideal resolution formula and asymptotic subadditivity give\n\\[\ns\\sigma_u(t)-A_X(u)\\le u(\\mathcal J_s(t))\\le s\\sigma_u(t).\n\\]\nCombine stabilization, \\(\\lambda_i\\to0\\), and the preceding inequalities. Subtract consecutive \\(E_i\\)-bounds to obtain the \\(\\Delta_i\\)-estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Proposition 3.2, “Countability of finite limiting changes.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is one countable set \\(\\mathcal E\\subset\\mathbb R\\) containing every finite limit of \\(E_{i_a}(u_a)\\), where \\(i_a\\to\\infty\\), the \\(u_a\\) are integral-valued divisorial valuations, and \\(A_X(u_a)\\le C\\) for a …", "statement": "<p>There is one countable set \\(\\mathcal E\\subset\\mathbb R\\) containing every finite limit of \\(E_{i_a}(u_a)\\), where \\(i_a\\to\\infty\\), the \\(u_a\\) are integral-valued divisorial valuations, and \\(A_X(u_a)\\le C\\) for a sequence-dependent constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose countable algebraically closed \\(F\\subset\\mathbb C\\) defining \\(X\\) and all \\(\\mathcal J_s\\). Positive log canonical thresholds of zero and pole divisors bound every \\(u_a(f)\\), \\(f\\in F(X_F)^*\\), by a constant times \\(A_X(u_a)\\). Integral-valuedness and diagonal extraction make each eventually constant, defining an integral valuation \\(u_\\infty\\) trivial on \\(F\\).</p><p>It is trivial or divisorial. Otherwise repeatedly blow up the generic smooth centre of codimension at least two. Each positive integral centre-ideal value increases the relative Jacobian value by at least one. After \\(N\\) such steps, choose a smooth affine neighbourhood of the last centre with Jacobian equation \\(b\\), giving \\(u_\\infty(b)\\ge N\\). Finitely many \\(F\\)-algebra generators eventually have their nonnegative limiting values under \\(u_a\\). Since \\(u_a\\) is trivial on \\(\\mathbb C\\), it is centred on the complexified chart. Hence\n\\[\nA_X(u_a)\\ge u_a(b)=u_\\infty(b)\\ge N,\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(N&gt;C\\). A codimension-one centre gives a positive integral multiple of its DVR order.</p><p>There are countably many \\(F\\)-models and prime divisors, hence countably many possible \\(u_\\infty\\). Finite chart generators give\n\\[\nu_a(\\mathcal J_s)=u_\\infty((\\mathcal J_s)_F)=a_s\n\\]\neventually. If \\(E_{i_a}(u_a)\\to e\\), Lemma 14.1 gives\n\\[\na_s/s\\le e\\le(a_s+C)/s\n\\]\nfor every \\(s\\). One \\(u_\\infty\\) determines at most one limit, independently of the sequence’s bound. Thus the set of all limits is countable.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "Proposition 3.2, “A common canonical index.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is one positive integer \\(R\\) such that \\(RK_{X_i}\\) is Cartier for every \\(i\\).", "statement": "<p>There is one positive integer \\(R\\) such that \\(RK_{X_i}\\) is Cartier for every \\(i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Otherwise choose points with local canonical indices \\(r_i\\to\\infty\\). The index-one cover is obtained by writing \\(rK=\\operatorname{div}(g)\\), adjoining \\(z^r=g\\), and normalizing. Minimality gives degree \\(r\\). It is quasi-étale and klt, and\n\\[\n\\theta=\\pi^*\\eta/z\n\\]\nis a zero-divisor canonical generator of primitive cyclic character. Klt Cohen–Macaulayness makes the cover Gorenstein.</p><p>There is one point over the chosen point. If the cyclic orbit had more than one, its permutation representation would give a regular semi-invariant unit \\(a\\) of character \\(z^j\\), \\(0&lt;j&lt;r\\). The invariant \\(a/z^j\\) would make \\(jK\\) principal near the base point, contradicting minimality.</p><p>Fix arbitrary \\(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\). Choose group elements so that the primitive \\(\\theta_i\\)-character angles tend to \\(\\alpha\\). Proposition 13 gives valuations \\(w_i'\\) with bounded discrepancies and integral-valued restrictions \\(u_i\\) to the common downstairs field. Quasi-étale compatibility gives\n\\[\n0&lt;A_X(u_i)\\le A_i(u_i)=A_{\\theta_i}(w_i')\\le C.\n\\]\nChoose a rational top form \\(\\eta_i^0\\) generating the canonical sheaf at the centre on smooth \\(X\\). Then\n\\[\nE_i(u_i)=w_i'(\\theta_i/\\eta_i^0).\n\\]\nThe residues of these bounded nonnegative numbers tend, on the selected subsequence, to \\(\\ell\\alpha\\). A further real limit belongs to the fixed countable \\(\\mathcal E\\) of Lemma 14.2. Therefore every \\(\\alpha\\) lies in\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{\\ell\\ge1}\\{\\alpha:\\ell\\alpha\\in\\overline{\\mathcal E}\\},\n\\]\na countable subset of the circle. This is impossible.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.4", "orig": "Proposition 3.2, “Vanishing on late flips.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed positive integer \\(m\\), every sufficiently late negative flipping contraction \\(f_i:X_i\\to Z_i\\) satisfies \\( R^kf_{i*}\\mathcal O_{X_i}(mK_{X_i})=0 \\qquad(k>0). \\)", "statement": "<p>For every fixed positive integer \\(m\\), every sufficiently late negative flipping contraction \\(f_i:X_i\\to Z_i\\) satisfies\n\\[\nR^kf_{i*}\\mathcal O_{X_i}(mK_{X_i})=0\n\\qquad(k&gt;0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On a sufficiently high common smooth resolution \\(p:U\\to X_i\\), \\(q:U\\to X_{i+1}\\), put\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_m=\n\\mathcal O_U(K_U+\\lceil(m-1)q^*K_{X_{i+1}}\\rceil).\n\\]\nThe rational divisor \\((m-1)q^*K_{X_{i+1}}\\) is nef and big over both \\(X_i\\) and \\(Z_i\\), including \\(m=1\\) because these maps are birational. Relative Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing gives zero higher direct images.</p><p>One has \\(p_*\\mathcal L_m=\\mathcal O_{X_i}(mK_{X_i})\\). One inclusion follows in codimension one. For a rational local section \\(h\\) of the right side,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}_U(h)+mp^*K_{X_i}\\ge0.\n\\]\nBefore rounding, the difference from the divisor defining \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) has coefficient\n\\[\nA_i-1-(m-1)\\Delta_i.\n\\]\nFor \\(i\\ge i(m)\\), Lemma 14.1 gives\n\\[\nA_i-1-(m-1)\\Delta_i\n\\ge-1+A_X/m&gt;-1.\n\\]\nRounding therefore leaves every integral section coefficient nonnegative. The pushforward identity and Leray give the stated vanishing.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 3.3, Relative weak Zariski decompositions", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(K_X\\) admits such a decomposition with both \\(P,N\\) rational Cartier.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be smooth complex quasi-projective and projective over a normal quasi-projective \\(Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(K_X\\) is pseudo-effective over \\(Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An NQC weak Zariski decomposition of \\(D\\) over \\(Z\\) is\n  \\[\n  p^*D\\equiv_ZP+N\n  \\]\n  on a projective birational model, where \\(N\\ge0\\) and \\(P\\) is a nonnegative real combination of rational Cartier divisors nef over \\(Z\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(K_X\\) admits such a decomposition with both \\(P,N\\) rational Cartier.</p>", "proof": "<p>Replace \\(Z\\) by the normal Stein base; this changes neither contracted curves nor relative numerical properties. Very general fibres are smooth projective with pseudo-effective canonical divisor: restrict a countable sequence of relative effective approximations outside their countably many bad loci. BDPP makes those fibres non-uniruled.</p><p>Compactify and resolve away from \\(X\\), obtaining a smooth projective \\(\\overline X\\to\\overline Z\\). If the base has dimension zero, use Proposition 14. Otherwise resolve \\(\\widetilde Z\\to\\overline Z\\), choose sufficiently positive \\(H\\), and take a general branch divisor in \\(|2H|\\). Bertini makes its pullbacks smooth nonempty reduced divisors. The double covers \\(Z^\\sharp\\) and \\(X^\\sharp\\) are smooth integral, with\n\\[\nK_{Z^\\sharp}\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}\\pi_Z^*(K_{\\widetilde Z}+r^*H),\n\\qquad\nK_{X^\\sharp}\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}\\pi^*(K_{\\overline X}+\\overline f^*H).\n\\]\nThe first is big for sufficiently positive \\(H\\). A covering family of rational curves on \\(X^\\sharp\\) would cover its non-uniruled base or its very general non-uniruled fibre. Thus \\(X^\\sharp\\) is non-uniruled, and BDPP makes its canonical class pseudo-effective.</p><p>Proposition 14 gives a nef-plus-effective rational Cartier decomposition of the pullback of \\(K_{X^\\sharp}\\). On a common equivariant resolution of this model and its conjugate, average the decomposition under the covering involution:\n\\[\ng^*\\pi^*(K_{\\overline X}+\\overline f^*H)=\\overline P+\\overline N.\n\\]\nBoth summands are invariant, \\(\\overline P\\) is nef, and \\(\\overline N\\ge0\\).</p><p>Take the finite quotient \\(q:T\\to Y\\), birational to \\(\\overline X\\). Invariant rational divisors descend by dividing prime coefficients by ramification indices. Rational Cartierness descends by the finite norm of a local equation on a semilocal neighbourhood of the orbit. Nefness descends by lifting curves; effectivity descends coefficientwise. Finite pullback is injective, so the equality descends. Restrict above \\(X\\); the base pullback term is numerically trivial over \\(Z\\), giving the required decomposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Section 3.3, Generalized pair and exact output", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a finite MMP starting on the original \\(X\\), without a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factoriality assumption, whose output satisfies all conclusions of Theorem 1 over \\(\\mathbb C\\), including non-extraction, exact discrepancy …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((X,B+M)\\) satisfy Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Rational nef data are NQC.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the relative minimal-model and terminating-MMP theorems of Tsakanikas–Xie in the precise roles specified in the paper.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite MMP starting on the original \\(X\\), without a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factoriality assumption, whose output satisfies all conclusions of Theorem 1 over \\(\\mathbb C\\), including non-extraction, exact discrepancy comparison, strictness for contracted original divisors, and relative Picard number one in the fibre case.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on dimension using Proposition 15 and Tsakanikas–Xie’s Theorem D: lower-dimensional smooth relative existence upgrades an NQC weak Zariski decomposition to a minimal model. This gives relative minimal models for all pseudo-effective smooth varieties. Their Theorem B then gives minimal models for all pseudo-effective NQC generalized lc pairs, and their Theorem 2.7 identifies this existence with the Birkar–Shokurov sense.</p><p>For the given rational generalized pair, their Theorem A applies either by this existence or by non-pseudo-effectivity. Choose an effective sufficiently positive ample rational \\(A\\) with\n\\[\n(X,B+A+M)\\text{ generalized lc},\n\\qquad K_X+B+A+M\\text{ nef}.\n\\]\nOn a fixed resolution carrying the nef data, general basepoint-free pullback and small coefficients preserve boundary coefficients at most one, while the ample class can be sufficiently large. This supplies the required terminating MMP from \\(X\\).</p><p>Every adjoint remains \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier: the rational Weil divisor obtained is real Cartier, and a rational divisor in a real span of Cartier divisors lies in their rational span by a finite rational linear system.</p><p>For a negative birational step \\(S\\dashrightarrow S^+\\) over \\(T\\), on a common resolution carrying the fixed nef part, write\n\\[\nF=p^*D-q^*D^+.\n\\]\nIt is \\(q\\)-exceptional and anti-nef over \\(q\\), hence \\(F\\ge0\\). Its coefficient is positive along every divisor of \\(S\\) contracted in the step. Otherwise, through a general point of that divisor outside \\(\\operatorname{Supp}F\\), take a curve in its positive-dimensional \\(q\\)-fibre. Effectivity gives \\(F\\cdot C\\ge0\\), but negativity of \\(D\\) gives \\(F\\cdot C&lt;0\\).</p><p>The same nef divisor occurs on the common resolution, so\n\\[\nA_{S,B_S+M_S}(u)\\le\nA_{S^+,B_{S^+}+M_{S^+}}(u).\n\\]\nStrictness occurs at the first step contracting an original divisor and persists. Positive morphisms are small, so the composite inverse extracts no divisors. Generalized lc follows.</p><p>At a fibre endpoint \\(h:Y\\to T\\), all vertical curves span the contracted ray and \\(-D_Y\\) is relatively ample. To justify the relative numerical rank without factoriality, let Cartier \\(L\\) have degree zero on the ray. Then \\(L-D_Y\\) is relatively ample. Choose \\(H\\) ample on \\(T\\) and effective small-general-coefficient\n\\[\nA\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}L+h^*H-D_Y\n\\]\nso that generalized lc is preserved. The new adjoint is nef over \\(T\\). Tsakanikas–Xie’s Theorem F gives a good minimal model; since it is already nef, no negative step occurs, so \\(L+h^*H\\) is relatively semiample.</p><p>A relatively semiample divisor numerically trivial over \\(T\\) contracts each connected projective fibre to a point. Its normal image is finite birational over \\(T\\), hence equals \\(T\\). Thus \\(L\\) is numerically a pullback. The hyperplane defined by a contracted curve is exactly \\(h^*N^1(T)_{\\mathbb R}\\), proving\n\\[\n\\rho(Y/T)=1,\\qquad \\rho(Y)-\\rho(T)=1.\n\\]\nThe same reasoning treats a real semiample sum termwise.</p><p>Finally the effective exceptional pullback differences preserve pseudo-effectivity through each birational step. A pseudo-effective adjoint cannot restrict anti-amply to a general positive-dimensional fibre. Conversely, a nef endpoint plus those effective differences makes the original adjoint pseudo-effective. This identifies the two alternatives.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Proposition 3.4, Descent to arbitrary characteristic-zero fields", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The finite complex MMP and its exact output descend and extend to give all conclusions of Theorem 1 over \\(k\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be any algebraically closed field of characteristic zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let the generalized rational pair and fixed nef data satisfy Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">The complex conclusion is supplied by Proposition 16.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite complex MMP and its exact output descend and extend to give all conclusions of Theorem 1 over \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Descend the input, nef data, and a log resolution to a countable algebraically closed \\(F\\subset k\\), and embed \\(F\\) into \\(\\mathbb C\\). Generalized lc is checked on that resolution; nefness is preserved under algebraically closed extension by spreading and specializing curves. Apply Proposition 16.</p><p>Descend each contraction. Suppose the current \\(S,D\\) are defined over \\(F\\). Pull back a very ample line bundle from the complex contraction target and spread it with generating sections over an integral finite-type \\(F\\)-scheme. Shrink so every fibre is generated, specialize at an \\(F\\)-point, and take the Stein contraction. On the connected complex parameter base, degrees on every fixed curve are constant. Thus the specialized contraction contracts exactly the same curves as the complex one.</p><p>The targets are isomorphic: each morphism is constant on every connected projective fibre of the other, since a positive-dimensional image would yield a curve not contracted by both. The normalized joint image has finite birational projections to the normal targets. Birationality follows because a contraction target’s function field is algebraically closed in \\(\\mathbb C(S)\\). Hence the projections are isomorphisms.</p><p>Recover a birational positive model by\n\\[\nS^+=\\operatorname{Proj}_T\n\\bigoplus_{m\\ge0}f_*\\mathcal O_S(mbD)\n\\]\nfor sufficiently divisible \\(b\\). Over \\(\\mathbb C\\), the effective exceptional difference identifies this algebra with that of the relatively ample positive adjoint. In the divisorial case its relative Proj is \\(T\\).</p><p>Divisorial sheaves and multiplication commute with the field extension: use their reflexive extension from a regular codimension-one open and the geometrically regular flat extension. Cartierness of \\(bD\\) descends faithfully flatly. Proper flat base change identifies the graded pieces. A common bound on generator degrees over a finite affine cover upstairs descends: the subalgebra generated in those degrees becomes the whole algebra after faithful flat extension, so each cokernel vanishes. Thus finite generation and relative Proj descend. Inductively this descends the whole finite diagram to \\(F\\).</p><p>Extend to \\(k\\). Projectivity, normality, birationality, non-extraction, relative ampleness, and the contraction equality \\(h_*\\mathcal O=\\mathcal O\\) persist. Every line bundle over an algebraically closed extension is numerically equivalent to one over \\(F\\), by spreading and specializing on a geometrically connected parameter base. Conversely, spread a relative curve and its target point in a flat projective family; specialization preserves intersection degrees and gives an effective vertical cycle. Hence nefness, numerical triviality, relative numerical spaces, and relative Picard ranks are unchanged.</p><p>Finally extend common smooth resolutions carrying the original nef data. Effective pullback differences and their positive coefficients along contracted original primes remain so. They imply all discrepancy comparisons and generalized lc over \\(k\\). The boundary and nef part are the required pushforward and original b-divisor trace. The same pullback differences and general-fibre argument identify the pseudo-effective and Mori alternatives over \\(k\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 18, "cite"], [2, 21, "cite"], [2, 23, "cite"], [2, 26, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 5, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [9, 18, "cite"], [9, 19, "cite"], [9, 20, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 9, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 20, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [17, 23, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [22, 26, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 35, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [26, 24, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [27, 31, "cite"], [27, 34, "cite"], [27, 36, "cite"], [28, 27, "cite"], [29, 27, "cite"], [30, 27, "cite"], [31, 0, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [32, 35, "cite"], [33, 35, "cite"], [34, 31, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [35, 31, "cite"], [36, 0, "cite"], [36, 40, "cite"], [36, 46, "cite"], [36, 49, "cite"], [37, 40, "cite"], [37, 46, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"], [38, 41, "cite"], [38, 47, "cite"], [39, 36, "cite"], [40, 0, "cite"], [40, 46, "cite"], [40, 48, "cite"], [41, 40, "part"], [42, 44, "cite"], [43, 44, "cite"], [44, 40, "cite"], [45, 0, "cite"], [45, 53, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [46, 48, "cite"], [47, 45, "cite"], [48, 49, "cite"], [49, 45, "cite"], [50, 0, "cite"], [50, 55, "cite"], [51, 50, "cite"], [51, 52, "cite"], [51, 54, "cite"], [52, 50, "cite"], [52, 53, "cite"], [53, 50, "cite"], [54, 50, "cite"], [55, 0, "cite"], [55, 56, "cite"], [56, 0, "cite"], [56, 57, "cite"], [57, 0, "cite"]]});
